(window.CONTENT_PARTS = window.CONTENT_PARTS || {})["questions-primary"] = [{"id":"pa-aw-001","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_25"],"note":"anat-airway","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which nerve provides sensory innervation to the laryngeal mucosa above the vocal cords?</p>","options":["External branch of the superior laryngeal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve","Recurrent laryngeal nerve"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>The <strong>internal laryngeal nerve</strong> (branch of the superior laryngeal nerve, vagus) pierces the thyrohyoid membrane and supplies the mucosa <strong>above</strong> the cords. Below the cords, the <strong>recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong> supplies sensation. The external branch is motor to cricothyroid.</p>"},{"id":"pa-aw-002","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_25"],"note":"anat-airway","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>After total thyroidectomy, a patient develops inspiratory stridor and respiratory distress on extubation, with both vocal cords lying close to the midline.</p>\n<p>Which injury is most likely?</p>","options":["Bilateral external laryngeal nerve injury","Bilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve injury","Hypoglossal nerve injury","Phrenic nerve injury","Unilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve injury"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The recurrent laryngeal nerves supply all intrinsic muscles except cricothyroid, including the <strong>only abductor (posterior cricoarytenoid)</strong>. <strong>Bilateral</strong> injury leaves the cords adducted, causing stridor and obstruction. Unilateral injury usually causes hoarseness.</p>"},{"id":"pa-aw-003","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_39","1_GA_K_5"],"note":"anat-airway","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Between which structures does the cricothyroid membrane lie?</p>","options":["Hyoid bone and thyroid cartilage","Thyroid cartilage and cricoid cartilage","Cricoid cartilage and first tracheal ring","Epiglottis and thyroid cartilage","Second and third tracheal rings"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The <strong>cricothyroid membrane</strong> lies between the inferior border of the <strong>thyroid cartilage</strong> and the superior border of the <strong>cricoid cartilage</strong>; it is the site for emergency front-of-neck access. The thyrohyoid membrane (A) is above.</p>"},{"id":"pa-tr-001","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_1"],"note":"anat-trachea-lungs","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which feature best explains why an endotracheal tube advanced too far usually enters the right main bronchus?</p>","options":["The left bronchus is shorter","The right bronchus is narrower","The right main bronchus is wider, shorter and more vertical","The carina lies to the left of the midline","The trachea deviates to the right"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The <strong>right main bronchus</strong> is wider, shorter and <strong>more in line with the trachea</strong> (about 25° from vertical versus about 45° on the left), so tubes and aspirated material tend to enter it.</p>"},{"id":"pa-tr-002","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_40","1_ICM_B_1"],"note":"anat-trachea-lungs","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>When inserting an intercostal chest drain in the triangle of safety, why should the needle or blunt dissection pass just above the upper border of a rib?</p>","options":["The intercostal muscles are thinnest there","The lung is furthest from the chest wall there","The neurovascular bundle runs along the lower border of each rib","The pleura is absent at the upper border","To avoid the internal thoracic artery"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The intercostal <strong>vein, artery and nerve</strong> run in the <strong>subcostal groove</strong> along the <strong>lower</strong> border of each rib, so going over the top of the rib below protects them.</p>"},{"id":"pa-heart-001","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_7"],"note":"anat-heart-vessels","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient develops ST elevation in leads II, III and aVF with complete heart block.</p>\n<p>Which coronary artery is most likely occluded?</p>","options":["Circumflex artery in a left-dominant system only","Left anterior descending artery","Left main stem","Obtuse marginal branch","Right coronary artery"],"answer":4,"explain":"<p>Inferior ST elevation (II, III, aVF) usually indicates <strong>right coronary artery</strong> occlusion, and the RCA supplies the <strong>AV node</strong> in most people (right dominance), explaining the heart block.</p>"},{"id":"pa-vein-001","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_36"],"note":"anat-heart-vessels","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>In the neck, what is the usual relationship of the internal jugular vein to the common carotid artery?</p>","options":["Directly anterior","Lateral (and slightly anterior)","Medial","Posterior","Separate from the carotid sheath"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Within the <strong>carotid sheath</strong>, the IJV lies <strong>lateral</strong> (often slightly anterior) to the common carotid artery, with the vagus nerve posteriorly between them. Anatomy varies, which is why ultrasound guidance is recommended.</p>"},{"id":"pa-vein-002","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_37"],"note":"anat-heart-vessels","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>In the femoral triangle just below the inguinal ligament, what is the order of structures from lateral to medial?</p>","options":["Artery, nerve, vein","Nerve, artery, vein","Nerve, vein, artery","Vein, artery, nerve","Vein, nerve, artery"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>From lateral to medial: <strong>nerve, artery, vein</strong> (then the femoral canal with lymphatics) — \"NAVY\". The femoral vein is medial to the pulse.</p>"},{"id":"pa-sp-001","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_33"],"note":"anat-spine-neuraxis","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>At what vertebral level does the spinal cord usually end in an adult?</p>","options":["T10","L1–L2","L3–L4","L5","S2"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The adult conus medullaris usually ends at <strong>L1–L2</strong> (it can be as low as L3). At birth it is at about L3. The dural sac ends at about <strong>S2</strong> in adults.</p>"},{"id":"pa-sp-002","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_32"],"note":"anat-spine-neuraxis","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During a midline epidural insertion, which ligament gives the characteristic increased resistance just before entering the epidural space?</p>","options":["Anterior longitudinal ligament","Interspinous ligament","Ligamentum flavum","Posterior longitudinal ligament","Supraspinous ligament"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The <strong>ligamentum flavum</strong> is dense and elastic, giving the firm resistance felt before the sudden loss of resistance on entering the epidural space.</p>"},{"id":"pa-sp-003","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_10"],"note":"anat-spine-neuraxis","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>After aortic aneurysm repair, a patient has paraplegia and loss of pain and temperature sensation below T10, with preserved proprioception and vibration sense.</p>\n<p>Which vessel is most likely to have been compromised?</p>","options":["Anterior spinal artery supply (artery of Adamkiewicz)","Basilar artery","Internal iliac artery","Posterior spinal arteries","Vertebral artery"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>This is <strong>anterior spinal artery syndrome</strong>: loss of the anterior two-thirds of the cord (motor and spinothalamic tracts) with preserved <strong>dorsal columns</strong> (supplied by the posterior spinal arteries). The <strong>artery of Adamkiewicz</strong> (usually T9–T12, left) reinforces the anterior spinal artery in the lower cord.</p>"},{"id":"pa-derm-001","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_15"],"note":"anat-spine-neuraxis","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which dermatome is at the level of the umbilicus?</p>","options":["T4","T6","T8","T10","L1"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p><strong>T10</strong> is at the umbilicus. T4 is at the nipples, T6 the xiphisternum, and L1 the inguinal ligament.</p>"},{"id":"pa-bp-001","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_16"],"note":"anat-plexuses","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient has weakness of wrist and finger extension after a fracture of the shaft of the humerus.</p>\n<p>Which nerve is most likely injured?</p>","options":["Axillary nerve","Median nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Radial nerve","Ulnar nerve"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>The <strong>radial nerve</strong> runs in the spiral groove of the humerus and is vulnerable in shaft fractures, causing <strong>wrist drop</strong>.</p>"},{"id":"pa-bp-002","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_16","1_RA_E_1"],"note":"anat-plexuses","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>An axillary brachial plexus block is performed by injecting around the axillary artery. The patient later has an area of numbness on the lateral forearm that remains sensate.</p>\n<p>Which nerve was probably missed?</p>","options":["Median nerve","Medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm","Musculocutaneous nerve","Radial nerve","Ulnar nerve"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The <strong>musculocutaneous nerve</strong> (from the lateral cord) leaves the sheath high in the axilla and lies within coracobrachialis, so it must be blocked separately. Its terminal branch, the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm, supplies the lateral forearm.</p>"},{"id":"pa-ls-001","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_19"],"note":"anat-plexuses","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>After a long procedure in the lithotomy position, a patient cannot dorsiflex the ankle and has numbness over the dorsum of the foot.</p>\n<p>Which nerve is most likely injured?</p>","options":["Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Common peroneal (fibular) nerve","Sural nerve","Tibial nerve"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The <strong>common peroneal nerve</strong> is compressed at the <strong>fibular head</strong> by stirrups, causing <strong>foot drop</strong> and numbness over the dorsum of the foot.</p>"},{"id":"pa-ls-002","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_18"],"note":"anat-plexuses","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>In which plane do the nerves targeted by a transversus abdominis plane (TAP) block run?</p>","options":["Between external and internal oblique","Between internal oblique and transversus abdominis","Between transversus abdominis and the peritoneum","Deep to the rectus abdominis in the posterior rectus sheath","Superficial to external oblique"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The anterior rami of T7–L1 run in the <strong>neurovascular plane between internal oblique and transversus abdominis</strong>, the target of the TAP block.</p>"},{"id":"pa-eye-001","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_26","1_GA_G1_24"],"note":"anat-head-neck-eye","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During strabismus surgery a child's heart rate falls from 110 to 45 beats per minute when the surgeon pulls on the medial rectus.</p>\n<p>What are the afferent and efferent limbs of this reflex?</p>","options":["Afferent facial, efferent vagus","Afferent oculomotor, efferent sympathetic","Afferent optic, efferent vagus","Afferent trigeminal, efferent vagus","Afferent vagus, efferent trigeminal"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>The <strong>oculocardiac reflex</strong>: afferents via the <strong>trigeminal nerve</strong> (ophthalmic division, ciliary ganglion) to the brainstem, efferents via the <strong>vagus</strong> to the heart. Stop the traction; give atropine or glycopyrronium if it persists.</p>"},{"id":"pa-cn-001","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_G1_24"],"note":"anat-head-neck-eye","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with a severe post-dural puncture headache develops double vision on looking laterally.</p>\n<p>Which cranial nerve is most likely affected?</p>","options":["Oculomotor (III)","Trochlear (IV)","Trigeminal (V)","Abducens (VI)","Facial (VII)"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>The <strong>abducens nerve</strong> has a long intracranial course and is stretched by downward sagging of the brain with low CSF pressure, causing lateral rectus palsy.</p>"},{"id":"pc-preop-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_POM_B_6"],"note":"preop-assessment","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes, stable angina and a BMI of 36 is listed for elective hernia repair. They manage two flights of stairs slowly.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate ASA physical status?</p>","options":["ASA I","ASA II","ASA III","ASA IV","ASA V"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Several diseases with <strong>functional limitation</strong> (poorly controlled diabetes, angina, obesity) indicate <strong>severe systemic disease: ASA III</strong>. ASA IV would require disease that is a constant threat to life (e.g. recent MI or unstable angina).</p>"},{"id":"pc-preop-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_POM_B_6","1_GA_H_5"],"note":"preop-assessment","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with a strangulated hernia and signs of bowel ischaemia needs surgery within hours.</p>\n<p>Which NCEPOD category applies?</p>","options":["Elective","Expedited","Immediate","Scheduled","Urgent"],"answer":4,"explain":"<p><strong>Urgent</strong>: acute onset or deterioration of conditions that threaten life, limb or organ survival, needing intervention within hours. <strong>Immediate</strong> is minutes (e.g. ruptured aneurysm); <strong>expedited</strong> is days.</p>"},{"id":"pc-aw-001","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_K_1"],"note":"airway-assessment","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>At laryngoscopy only the epiglottis can be seen, with no part of the glottis visible.</p>\n<p>What is the Cormack–Lehane grade?</p>","options":["1","2a","2b","3","4"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p><strong>Grade 3</strong>: epiglottis only. Grade 4: no laryngeal structures. Grade 2: part of the glottis or arytenoids visible (2a/2b in the modified version).</p>"},{"id":"pc-rsi-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_B_9","1_GA_G3_52"],"note":"rsi-aspiration","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with a history of malignant hyperthermia needs a rapid sequence induction for an acute appendicectomy.</p>\n<p>Which neuromuscular blocker is most appropriate?</p>","options":["Atracurium 0.5 mg/kg","Mivacurium 0.2 mg/kg","Rocuronium 1.2 mg/kg","Suxamethonium 1.5 mg/kg","Vecuronium 0.1 mg/kg"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p><strong>Suxamethonium is a trigger</strong> for MH and must be avoided. <strong>High-dose rocuronium</strong> gives intubating conditions in about 60 seconds and can be reversed rapidly with sugammadex.</p>"},{"id":"pc-cico-001","subject":"anatomy","codes":["1_GA_K_4","1_GA_K_5"],"note":"difficult-airway-cico","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>After failed intubation and failed rescue with a second-generation supraglottic airway and facemask, the patient's SpO<sub>2</sub> is 70% and falling.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate next step?</p>","options":["A further attempt at direct laryngoscopy by the same operator","Declare \"can't intubate, can't oxygenate\" and perform emergency front-of-neck access","Insert a nasopharyngeal airway and wait for spontaneous breathing","Needle cricothyroidotomy with low-pressure oxygen","Wait for sugammadex to take effect before any other intervention"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>When oxygenation cannot be maintained by any route, <strong>declare CICO</strong> and perform <strong>front-of-neck access</strong> (scalpel–bougie–tube cricothyroidotomy in the DAS approach) without delay. Repeated attempts cause trauma and waste time; reversal may not restore a patent airway.</p>"},{"id":"pc-ext-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_E_12","1_GA_B_24"],"note":"extubation-recovery","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A child develops complete laryngospasm after removal of a laryngeal mask. CPAP with 100% oxygen and jaw thrust have not helped and the SpO<sub>2</sub> is 75%.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate next drug treatment?</p>","options":["IV adrenaline","IV dexamethasone","IV propofol to deepen anaesthesia","Nebulised salbutamol","Oral paracetamol"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>After CPAP and jaw thrust, <strong>deepen anaesthesia with propofol</strong>; if laryngospasm persists, give a small dose of <strong>suxamethonium</strong> (with atropine in children for bradycardia) and intubate if needed.</p>"},{"id":"pc-inc-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_E_16"],"note":"critical-incidents","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During laparoscopic cholecystectomy immediately after insufflation, ETCO<sub>2</sub> falls abruptly, SpO<sub>2</sub> falls and a \"mill-wheel\" murmur is heard.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>","options":["Anaphylaxis","CO<sub>2</sub> gas embolism","Endobronchial intubation","Surgical emphysema","Vasovagal reflex"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Sudden cardiovascular collapse with a fall in ETCO<sub>2</sub> at insufflation, with a mill-wheel murmur, suggests <strong>gas embolism</strong> (misplaced Veress needle). Stop insufflation, release the pneumoperitoneum, 100% O<sub>2</sub>, head-down left lateral position, support the circulation and CPR if required.</p>"},{"id":"pc-inc-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_E_2","1_GA_E_15"],"note":"critical-incidents","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A ventilated trauma patient with rib fractures becomes hypotensive with rising airway pressures, reduced air entry on the left and tracheal deviation to the right.</p>\n<p>What is the immediate treatment?</p>","options":["Bronchodilators","Chest X-ray before any intervention","Decompression of the left chest (needle or finger thoracostomy)","Increase PEEP","Reposition the tracheal tube"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>This is <strong>tension pneumothorax</strong>, a clinical diagnosis. Decompress immediately (finger thoracostomy in a ventilated patient, or needle decompression), then insert a chest drain. Do not wait for imaging.</p>"},{"id":"pc-anaph-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_E_18"],"note":"anaphylaxis-management","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Two minutes after co-amoxiclav is given at induction, an adult patient's blood pressure falls to 55/30 mmHg with bronchospasm.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate first drug treatment under anaesthesia with IV access?</p>","options":["Chlorphenamine 10 mg IV","Hydrocortisone 200 mg IV","Adrenaline IV in titrated boluses (for example 50 micrograms)","Adrenaline 1 mg IV as a single bolus","Salbutamol 5 mg nebulised"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p><strong>Adrenaline</strong> is first-line. In an anaesthetised, monitored adult with IV access, give <strong>titrated IV boluses</strong> (e.g. 50 µg) and start an infusion if needed. 1 mg IV boluses are for cardiac arrest. Antihistamines and steroids are secondary.</p>"},{"id":"pc-last-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_RA_G_2","1_GA_E_22"],"note":"last-management","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During a femoral nerve block, a 70 kg patient becomes confused and then has a generalised seizure, followed by broad-complex bradycardia.</p>\n<p>What is the initial bolus dose of 20% lipid emulsion?</p>","options":["0.5 mL/kg","1.5 mL/kg","5 mL/kg","15 mL/kg","50 mL"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>AAGBI/QRH guidance: <strong>1.5 mL/kg</strong> of 20% lipid emulsion over about a minute (about 100 mL for 70 kg), then an infusion of 15 mL/kg/h, with repeat boluses and a doubled infusion rate if needed, up to a maximum of about 12 mL/kg.</p>"},{"id":"pc-neur-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_RA_D_1","1_GA_E_21"],"note":"neuraxial-basics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Five minutes after a spinal anaesthetic for caesarean section, the patient says her hands feel weak and she is finding it hard to breathe. Her heart rate is 48 beats per minute and blood pressure 70/35 mmHg.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely explanation?</p>","options":["Anaphylaxis to bupivacaine","Amniotic fluid embolism","High spinal block","Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity","Vasovagal syncope"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Hand weakness (<strong>C6–T1</strong>), dyspnoea, bradycardia (cardiac accelerator fibres <strong>T1–T4</strong> blocked) and hypotension indicate a <strong>high spinal</strong>. Support breathing (intubate if needed), give vasopressors and atropine, maintain uterine displacement, and reassure.</p>"},{"id":"pc-neur-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_RA_G_1"],"note":"neuraxial-basics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Two days after an epidural in labour with an accidental dural puncture, a woman has a headache that is severe when sitting and relieved by lying flat, with neck stiffness.</p>\n<p>After excluding other causes, what is the most effective treatment if conservative measures fail?</p>","options":["Caffeine infusion","Epidural blood patch","Intravenous fluids alone","Lumbar puncture to measure pressure","Sumatriptan"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>This is <strong>post-dural puncture headache</strong>. An <strong>epidural blood patch</strong> is the most effective treatment for significant or persistent symptoms. Always consider other causes (e.g. pre-eclampsia, cerebral venous thrombosis, subdural haematoma).</p>"},{"id":"pc-obs-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_R_1"],"note":"obstetric-basics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A woman with severe pre-eclampsia is receiving magnesium sulphate. Her knee reflexes have become absent and her respiratory rate is 8 breaths per minute.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate immediate treatment?</p>","options":["Increase the magnesium infusion","Intravenous calcium gluconate and stop the magnesium","Intravenous labetalol","Intravenous naloxone","Neostigmine"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Loss of reflexes and respiratory depression indicate <strong>magnesium toxicity</strong>. <strong>Stop the infusion</strong>, support breathing, and give <strong>IV calcium gluconate</strong> (a physiological antagonist). Check levels and renal function.</p>"},{"id":"pc-obs-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_Q_7"],"note":"obstetric-basics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A woman in labour has a prolonged fetal bradycardia to 60 beats per minute that does not recover.</p>\n<p>What category of caesarean section is this?</p>","options":["Category 1","Category 2","Category 3","Category 4","Elective"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>An <strong>immediate threat to the life of the fetus</strong> (or woman) is <strong>category 1</strong>. Category 2 is compromise that is not immediately life-threatening.</p>"},{"id":"pc-paed-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_O_3"],"note":"paeds-basics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>According to UK consensus guidance, what is the minimum fasting time for clear fluids before elective anaesthesia in children?</p>","options":["No fasting required","1 hour","2 hours","4 hours","6 hours"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The 2018 consensus endorsed by the APA and European societies allows clear fluids up to <strong>1 hour</strong> before elective anaesthesia in children, reducing dehydration, hypoglycaemia and distress without increasing aspiration.</p>"},{"id":"pc-paed-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_P_4"],"note":"paeds-basics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 20 kg child is having a caudal block with levobupivacaine 0.25% for an inguinal hernia repair. The maximum dose is taken as 2 mg/kg.</p>\n<p>What is the maximum volume that could be given?</p>","options":["4 mL","8 mL","16 mL","20 mL","40 mL"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>0.25% = 2.5 mg/mL. Maximum dose = 2 × 20 = 40 mg → 40 / 2.5 = <strong>16 mL</strong>. In practice 0.5–1 mL/kg (10–20 mL here) is used depending on the block height needed, always within the maximum dose.</p>"},{"id":"pc-als-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_RT_B_2","1_RT_B_3"],"note":"resuscitation-als","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>An adult in ventricular fibrillation has received three shocks with CPR between them.</p>\n<p>According to current UK ALS guidance, which drugs are given at this point?</p>","options":["Adenosine 6 mg and atropine 3 mg","Adrenaline 1 mg and amiodarone 300 mg","Adrenaline 0.5 mg and lidocaine 1 mg/kg","Amiodarone 150 mg only","Magnesium 2 g and calcium chloride 10 mL"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>In shockable rhythms, <strong>adrenaline 1 mg</strong> and <strong>amiodarone 300 mg</strong> are given after the <strong>third shock</strong>; adrenaline is then repeated every 3–5 minutes and a further 150 mg of amiodarone after the fifth shock.</p>"},{"id":"pc-tr-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_N_4"],"note":"trauma-transfer","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A head-injured patient opens their eyes to pain, makes incomprehensible sounds, and withdraws from pain.</p>\n<p>What is their Glasgow Coma Scale score?</p>","options":["6","7","8","9","10"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Eyes to pain = <strong>2</strong>; incomprehensible sounds = <strong>2</strong>; withdrawal from pain = <strong>4</strong>. Total <strong>8</strong>, an indication to consider intubation to protect the airway.</p>"},{"id":"pc-tr-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_N_4","1_RT_E_1"],"note":"trauma-transfer","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Before transferring a ventilated patient with a traumatic brain injury to a neurosurgical centre, which PaCO<sub>2</sub> target is recommended?</p>","options":["3.0–3.5 kPa","4.5–5.0 kPa","5.5–6.5 kPa","6.5–7.5 kPa","No target; ventilate to comfort"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The Association of Anaesthetists' guidance recommends <strong>PaCO<sub>2</sub> 4.5–5.0 kPa</strong>, with PaO<sub>2</sub> above 13 kPa and MAP above 80 mmHg. Aggressive hyperventilation risks cerebral ischaemia; hypercapnia raises ICP.</p>"},{"id":"pc-sep-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_ICM_E_1"],"note":"sepsis-critical-illness","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>According to Sepsis-3 definitions, which combination defines septic shock?</p>","options":["Fever, tachycardia and a raised white cell count","Lactate above 4 mmol/L alone","Sepsis requiring vasopressors to maintain MAP ≥ 65 mmHg with lactate &gt; 2 mmol/L despite adequate fluid resuscitation","Systolic blood pressure below 90 mmHg with a positive blood culture","Two or more SIRS criteria"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p><strong>Septic shock</strong> (Sepsis-3): sepsis with <strong>vasopressors needed to maintain MAP ≥ 65 mmHg</strong> and <strong>lactate &gt; 2 mmol/L</strong> despite adequate fluid resuscitation. SIRS criteria are no longer used for the definition.</p>"},{"id":"pc-dm-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_POM_C_3"],"note":"diabetes-perioperative","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with type 1 diabetes on a variable-rate insulin infusion after surgery has a capillary glucose of 3.1 mmol/L. He is drowsy but rousable.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate immediate action?</p>","options":["Continue the insulin infusion and recheck in an hour","Give 75–100 mL of 20% glucose intravenously and review the insulin infusion rate","Give glucagon intramuscularly and stop all insulin permanently","Give an oral sugary drink only","Stop the substrate fluid"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Treat hypoglycaemia promptly with <strong>IV glucose</strong> in a drowsy patient, recheck after 10–15 minutes, and <strong>reduce (not stop)</strong> the insulin infusion according to the protocol. <strong>Never stop all insulin in type 1 diabetes</strong> (ketoacidosis); basal insulin must continue.</p>"},{"id":"pc-cons-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_SG_F_1","1_POM_G_1"],"note":"consent-capacity-safeguarding","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Under the Mental Capacity Act 2005, which is required for a patient to have capacity to consent to anaesthesia?</p>","options":["Agreeing with the doctor's recommendation","Being able to understand, retain, use and weigh the information, and communicate a decision","Having no diagnosis of dementia","Having a relative present","Signing a consent form"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Capacity is <strong>decision-specific</strong>: the person must be able to <strong>understand, retain, use or weigh</strong> the relevant information and <strong>communicate</strong> a decision. An unwise decision does not in itself indicate incapacity.</p>"},{"id":"pc-hip-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_B_16"],"note":"elderly-hip-day-remote","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During a cemented hemiarthroplasty, the patient becomes hypoxic and hypotensive immediately after insertion of the cemented prosthesis.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>","options":["Anaphylaxis to latex","Bone cement implantation syndrome","High spinal block","Hypovolaemia from occult bleeding","Tension pneumothorax"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Bone cement implantation syndrome</strong>: hypoxia, hypotension, arrhythmias or arrest around cementation, from embolisation and mediator release raising pulmonary vascular resistance. Support with oxygen, fluids and vasopressors; communicate with the surgeon before cementing in high-risk patients.</p>"},{"id":"pc-mri-001","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_M_3"],"note":"elderly-hip-day-remote","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which item presents the greatest immediate danger in the MRI scanner room?</p>","options":["A ferromagnetic oxygen cylinder","A plastic cannula","A titanium hip prosthesis in the patient","An MR-conditional infusion pump used within its limits","Ear defenders"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>The powerful static magnetic field turns <strong>ferromagnetic</strong> objects such as steel oxygen cylinders into dangerous <strong>projectiles</strong>. Use MR-safe or MR-conditional equipment; titanium implants are usually safe but must be checked.</p>"},{"id":"pm-dmp-001","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F2_23","1_GA_F3_1"],"note":"measurement-principles","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A radial arterial trace shows a sharp, spiky systolic peak and marked \"ringing\" after a fast flush, with many oscillations before settling.</p>\n<p>What is the effect on the displayed values compared with the true pressures?</p>","options":["All values underestimated","Mean pressure greatly overestimated","Systolic overestimated, diastolic underestimated, mean relatively accurate","Systolic underestimated, diastolic overestimated","No effect on any value"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>This is an <strong>underdamped</strong> (resonant) system. Overshoot exaggerates the systolic peak and the trough, so <strong>systolic reads high and diastolic low</strong>, while the <strong>mean</strong> is relatively unaffected. An overdamped trace does the opposite (option D).</p>"},{"id":"pm-dmp-002","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F2_23"],"note":"measurement-principles","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which change will increase the natural (resonant) frequency of an invasive arterial pressure measuring system?</p>","options":["Adding a three-way tap and extension tubing","An air bubble in the tubing","Using a longer catheter","Using a shorter, stiffer, wider-bore line","Using compliant tubing"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Natural frequency rises with <strong>shorter, wider, stiffer</strong> tubing and a stiffer transducer diaphragm, and falls with long, narrow, compliant tubing, extra connections and air bubbles. A high natural frequency (well above the signal's harmonics) reduces distortion.</p>"},{"id":"pm-bp-001","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_11"],"note":"pressure-measurement","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Using an oscillometric non-invasive blood pressure device, which value is measured most directly and accurately?</p>","options":["Diastolic pressure","Mean arterial pressure","Pulse pressure","Systolic pressure","They are all measured equally directly"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The <strong>point of maximal oscillation</strong> of cuff pressure corresponds to <strong>mean arterial pressure</strong>. Systolic and diastolic values are <strong>calculated</strong> from it by algorithms.</p>"},{"id":"pm-bp-002","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_11"],"note":"pressure-measurement","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A non-invasive blood pressure cuff that is too narrow for the patient's arm is used.</p>\n<p>What is the likely effect?</p>","options":["Blood pressure is overestimated","Blood pressure is underestimated","Only the heart rate reading is affected","No effect with oscillometric devices","Only diastolic pressure is affected"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>A cuff that is <strong>too small</strong> needs a higher pressure to occlude the artery, so blood pressure is <strong>overestimated</strong>. The bladder width should be about 40% of arm circumference.</p>"},{"id":"pm-spo2-001","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_9"],"note":"pulse-oximetry","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which two wavelengths are used by a standard pulse oximeter?</p>","options":["460 nm and 560 nm","560 nm and 805 nm","660 nm and 940 nm","805 nm and 1300 nm","940 nm and 4300 nm"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p><strong>Red (660 nm)</strong>, absorbed more by deoxyhaemoglobin, and <strong>infrared (940 nm)</strong>, absorbed more by oxyhaemoglobin. 805 nm is an isobestic point; 4.3 µm is used for CO<sub>2</sub> capnography.</p>"},{"id":"pm-spo2-002","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_9"],"note":"pulse-oximetry","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which condition is associated with pulse oximetry overestimating the true arterial oxygen saturation, increasing the risk of undetected hypoxaemia?</p>","options":["Anaemia with Hb 90 g/L","Carbon monoxide poisoning","Intravenous methylene blue injection","Severe tricuspid regurgitation with venous pulsation","Hyperbilirubinaemia"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Carboxyhaemoglobin</strong> absorbs red light like oxyhaemoglobin, so the oximeter counts it as oxygenated: <strong>falsely high SpO<sub>2</sub></strong>. (Pulse oximetry also tends to overestimate saturation in people with darker skin.)</p>\n<ul><li><strong>C</strong> and <strong>D</strong> give falsely <strong>low</strong> readings.</li><li><strong>A</strong> Anaemia does not affect SpO<sub>2</sub> accuracy much.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pm-co2-001","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_8","1_GA_E_4"],"note":"capnography","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During a hip replacement, immediately after cement insertion, the ETCO<sub>2</sub> falls suddenly from 4.8 to 2.2 kPa with hypotension and desaturation. Ventilation is unchanged.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely cause?</p>","options":["Bronchospasm","Disconnection of the breathing system","Hyperventilation","Increased alveolar dead space from pulmonary embolism (bone cement implantation syndrome)","Malignant hyperthermia"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>A sudden fall in ETCO<sub>2</sub> with <strong>cardiovascular collapse</strong> after cementation suggests <strong>bone cement implantation syndrome</strong>: embolised material increases pulmonary vascular resistance and <strong>alveolar dead space</strong> and reduces cardiac output.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>B</strong> would give no trace at all.</li><li><strong>A</strong> changes the waveform shape (upsloping) rather than causing a sudden fall with collapse.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pm-co2-002","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_8"],"note":"capnography","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A capnograph plateau shows a notch (\"cleft\") during the alveolar phase in a patient being ventilated at the end of a case.</p>\n<p>What does this usually indicate?</p>","options":["Cardiac oscillations","Exhausted soda lime","Oesophageal intubation","Spontaneous respiratory efforts during mechanical ventilation","Sudden reduction in cardiac output"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>A <strong>curare cleft</strong> reflects <strong>spontaneous breathing efforts</strong> interrupting the plateau, often as neuromuscular block wears off.</p>"},{"id":"pm-gas-001","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_15"],"note":"gas-analysis","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which property of oxygen is used by the rapid-response oxygen analysers in most anaesthetic monitors?</p>","options":["Absorption of infrared light","Chemical reaction in a fuel cell","Paramagnetism","Raman scattering","Thermal conductivity"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Oxygen is <strong>paramagnetic</strong> (attracted into a magnetic field). Paramagnetic analysers give fast, breath-by-breath readings. O<sub>2</sub> does not absorb infrared because it is a diatomic molecule of a single element.</p>"},{"id":"pm-gas-002","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_13"],"note":"gas-analysis","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What is the principle of the Severinghaus electrode?</p>","options":["A glass pH electrode behind a CO<sub>2</sub>-permeable membrane, measuring the pH change in a bicarbonate solution","A platinum cathode reducing oxygen","Absorption of infrared light by CO<sub>2</sub>","A silver/silver chloride electrode measuring chloride","A thermistor measuring the heat of reaction"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>CO<sub>2</sub> diffuses across a membrane into a bicarbonate solution, changing its <strong>pH</strong>, which a glass electrode measures: the <strong>Severinghaus (PCO<sub>2</sub>) electrode</strong>. The Clark electrode (B) measures PO<sub>2</sub>.</p>"},{"id":"pm-temp-001","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_2"],"note":"temperature-measurement","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What is the principle of a thermocouple?</p>","options":["Expansion of a liquid in a capillary tube","Infrared emission from a body surface","A semiconductor whose resistance falls with temperature","A voltage generated at the junction of two dissimilar metals (Seebeck effect)","Linear increase in the resistance of a platinum wire"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>A <strong>thermocouple</strong> generates a small voltage proportional to the temperature difference between its measuring and reference junctions (<strong>Seebeck effect</strong>). C describes a thermistor; E a platinum resistance thermometer.</p>"},{"id":"pm-co-001","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_12"],"note":"cardiac-output-measurement","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient's oxygen consumption is 300 mL/min, arterial oxygen content 200 mL/L and mixed venous oxygen content 140 mL/L.</p>\n<p>What is the cardiac output by the Fick principle?</p>","options":["2.1 L/min","4.0 L/min","5.0 L/min","6.0 L/min","8.4 L/min"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>CO = VO<sub>2</sub> / (CaO<sub>2</sub> − CvO<sub>2</sub>) = 300 / (200 − 140) = 300 / 60 = <strong>5 L/min</strong>.</p>"},{"id":"pm-co-002","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_12"],"note":"cardiac-output-measurement","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During thermodilution measurement of cardiac output, the area under the temperature–time curve is smaller than on a previous measurement with the same injectate.</p>\n<p>What does this indicate?</p>","options":["Cardiac output has fallen","Cardiac output has risen","Tricuspid regurgitation has developed","The injectate was warmer","The thermistor has failed"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>By the <strong>Stewart–Hamilton</strong> equation, cardiac output is inversely proportional to the area under the indicator curve: faster flow washes the cold indicator past the thermistor more quickly, giving a <strong>smaller area and a higher output</strong>.</p>"},{"id":"pm-pft-001","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_16"],"note":"flow-volume-pft","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A flow–volume loop shows flattening of the inspiratory limb only, with a normal expiratory limb.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely cause?</p>","options":["Asthma","Fixed tracheal stenosis","Pulmonary fibrosis","Tracheomalacia within the thorax","Variable extrathoracic obstruction such as bilateral vocal cord palsy"],"answer":4,"explain":"<p>A <strong>variable extrathoracic</strong> obstruction narrows further during inspiration (negative airway pressure), flattening the <strong>inspiratory</strong> limb. Fixed obstruction flattens both limbs; variable intrathoracic obstruction flattens the expiratory limb.</p>"},{"id":"pm-pft-002","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_5"],"note":"flow-volume-pft","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What is the principle of a pneumotachograph?</p>","options":["A heated wire is cooled by gas flow","A rotating vane counts volume through gears","The pressure drop across a laminar-flow resistance is proportional to flow","Ultrasound transit times differ with flow direction","A bobbin rises in a tapered tube"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>A <strong>pneumotachograph</strong> passes gas through a resistance designed to keep flow <strong>laminar</strong>, so the <strong>pressure drop is proportional to flow</strong> (Hagen–Poiseuille). Integrating flow gives volume. A describes a hot-wire anemometer, B a Wright respirometer, E a rotameter.</p>"},{"id":"pm-nm-001","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_17"],"note":"nm-monitoring","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Twenty minutes after a large dose of rocuronium, there is no response to train-of-four stimulation.</p>\n<p>Which pattern of stimulation best quantifies the depth of block?</p>","options":["Double burst stimulation","Post-tetanic count","Single twitch at 1 Hz","Tetanic stimulation at 100 Hz","Train-of-four ratio"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>When the TOF count is zero, the <strong>post-tetanic count</strong> (single twitches after 5 s of 50 Hz tetanus) quantifies <strong>deep</strong> block: the fewer post-tetanic twitches, the deeper the block.</p>"},{"id":"pm-nm-002","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_17"],"note":"nm-monitoring","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What is the frequency and number of stimuli in a train-of-four?</p>","options":["4 stimuli at 1 Hz","4 stimuli at 2 Hz","4 stimuli at 50 Hz","4 stimuli at 0.1 Hz","2 bursts of 3 stimuli at 50 Hz"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>TOF = <strong>four supramaximal stimuli at 2 Hz</strong> (0.5 s apart). E describes double burst stimulation (DBS 3,3).</p>"},{"id":"pm-abg-001","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_ICM_F_1"],"note":"abg-interpretation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>An arterial blood gas shows pH 7.52, PaCO<sub>2</sub> 6.4 kPa, bicarbonate 38 mmol/L, potassium 2.8 mmol/L and chloride 88 mmol/L in a patient with pyloric obstruction.</p>\n<p>What is the primary disturbance?</p>","options":["Metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation","Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation","Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation","Respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Alkalaemia with a <strong>high bicarbonate</strong> is a <strong>metabolic alkalosis</strong>; the raised PaCO<sub>2</sub> is compensatory hypoventilation. Vomiting of gastric contents loses H<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>−</sup> (hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic alkalosis).</p>"},{"id":"pm-abg-002","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_ICM_F_1"],"note":"abg-interpretation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A ventilated patient has a PaO<sub>2</sub> of 12 kPa on an FiO<sub>2</sub> of 0.6.</p>\n<p>What is the P/F ratio and what does it indicate?</p>","options":["7.2 kPa — normal gas exchange","20 kPa — moderately impaired oxygenation","40 kPa — normal gas exchange","72 kPa — severe impairment","12 kPa — cannot be calculated without PaCO<sub>2</sub>"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>P/F = 12 / 0.6 = <strong>20 kPa</strong> (150 mmHg). Values below about 26.7 kPa (200 mmHg) indicate moderate impairment of oxygenation in the Berlin ARDS criteria (with the other criteria met).</p>"},{"id":"pm-ecg-001","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F1_12"],"note":"ecg-interpretation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>ST-segment changes on a theatre monitor are much less obvious than on a 12-lead ECG recorded minutes later.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely technical reason?</p>","options":["Electrode gel had dried","Monitoring mode filters a narrower bandwidth, distorting ST segments","The monitor uses a different paper speed","Theatre monitors cannot display chest leads","The patient was cooler in theatre"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>In <strong>monitoring mode</strong>, a narrow bandwidth (e.g. 0.5–40 Hz) reduces baseline wander and interference but <strong>distorts low-frequency components such as ST segments</strong>. <strong>Diagnostic mode</strong> (0.05–150 Hz) is needed for ST analysis.</p>"},{"id":"pm-ecg-002","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_G2_43"],"note":"ecg-interpretation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>An ECG shows a constant PR interval of 0.18 s with occasional P waves that are not followed by a QRS complex.</p>\n<p>What is the rhythm?</p>","options":["Complete heart block","First-degree heart block","Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) second-degree block","Mobitz type II second-degree block","Sinus arrhythmia"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Dropped beats <strong>without progressive PR lengthening</strong> indicate <strong>Mobitz II</strong> block, which can progress to complete heart block and may need pacing. In Mobitz I the PR interval lengthens progressively before a dropped beat.</p>"},{"id":"pm-img-001","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_G1_6"],"note":"imaging-interpretation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>On a supine chest X-ray after insertion of a right internal jugular central venous catheter, where should the catheter tip ideally lie?</p>","options":["In the right ventricle","In the subclavian vein","In the lower superior vena cava or at the cavo-atrial junction","In the left brachiocephalic vein","Beyond the carina in the right main bronchus"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The tip should lie in the <strong>lower SVC or at the cavo-atrial junction</strong> (roughly at or just below the level of the carina on the radiograph), parallel to the vessel wall, to reduce the risk of perforation and arrhythmias.</p>"},{"id":"pm-img-002","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_G1_6"],"note":"imaging-interpretation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A chest X-ray shows loss of the right heart border with preserved right hemidiaphragm outline.</p>\n<p>Which lobe is most likely consolidated?</p>","options":["Left lower lobe","Lingula","Right lower lobe","Right middle lobe","Right upper lobe"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>The <strong>silhouette sign</strong>: the right heart border is adjacent to the <strong>right middle lobe</strong>. Right lower lobe consolidation obscures the right hemidiaphragm; the lingula obscures the left heart border.</p>"},{"id":"pm-std-001","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_20"],"note":"monitoring-standards","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>According to the Association of Anaesthetists' 2021 monitoring standards, which additional monitoring is recommended when total intravenous anaesthesia is used together with neuromuscular blocking drugs?</p>","options":["Bispectral index or another processed EEG monitor","Central venous pressure","Cerebral oximetry","Invasive arterial pressure","Oesophageal Doppler"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>The 2021 standards recommend <strong>processed EEG (depth of anaesthesia) monitoring</strong> when TIVA is used with neuromuscular blockade, because the usual clinical signs of awareness are masked and there is no end-tidal agent concentration to guide dosing.</p>"},{"id":"pm-abg-003","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_ICM_F_1","1_GA_G2_8"],"note":"abg-interpretation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 45-year-old with severe diarrhoea has pH 7.28, PaCO<sub>2</sub> 3.8 kPa, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 14 mmol/L, Na<sup>+</sup> 138 mmol/L, Cl<sup>−</sup> 114 mmol/L.</p>\n<p>What is the anion gap (without potassium) and the likely disorder?</p>","options":["10 mmol/L; normal anion gap metabolic acidosis","10 mmol/L; respiratory acidosis","24 mmol/L; raised anion gap metabolic acidosis","38 mmol/L; lactic acidosis","124 mmol/L; hyperchloraemic alkalosis"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Anion gap = 138 − (114 + 14) = <strong>10 mmol/L</strong> (normal). Low bicarbonate with high chloride and a normal gap: <strong>hyperchloraemic (normal anion gap) metabolic acidosis</strong> from gastrointestinal bicarbonate loss, with respiratory compensation.</p>"},{"id":"pm-abg-004","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_ICM_F_1"],"note":"abg-interpretation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>An arterial sample is left at room temperature for an hour with an air bubble in the syringe before analysis.</p>\n<p>Which pattern of error is most likely?</p>","options":["PaO<sub>2</sub> falsely low and PaCO<sub>2</sub> falsely high only","PaO<sub>2</sub> moves towards the partial pressure in room air; PaCO<sub>2</sub> falls","pH falsely high due to lactate production","No change, because blood gases are stable","Potassium falls"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>An <strong>air bubble</strong> equilibrates with the sample: PO<sub>2</sub> moves towards about 20 kPa (so a low PaO<sub>2</sub> rises and a very high PaO<sub>2</sub> falls) and PCO<sub>2</sub> falls towards zero. Delay also allows ongoing cell metabolism (lowering PO<sub>2</sub> and pH). Samples should be analysed promptly without air.</p>"},{"id":"pm-ecg-003","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_G2_43","1_RT_B_1"],"note":"ecg-interpretation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient on a potassium-sparing diuretic with acute kidney injury has an ECG showing tall peaked T waves, a prolonged PR interval and widening QRS complexes.</p>\n<p>What is the most urgent treatment?</p>","options":["Calcium gluconate or chloride IV","Furosemide IV","Oral calcium resonium","Salbutamol nebuliser alone","Sodium bicarbonate orally"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>ECG changes of <strong>hyperkalaemia</strong> require immediate <strong>IV calcium</strong> to stabilise the myocardium (it does not lower potassium). Then shift potassium into cells (insulin–glucose, salbutamol) and remove it (dialysis, binders), and treat the cause.</p>"},{"id":"pm-ecg-004","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F1_12"],"note":"ecg-interpretation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which ECG lead combination is most commonly used in theatre to detect both arrhythmias and lateral left ventricular ischaemia?</p>","options":["aVR and V1","I and aVL","II and V5","III and aVF","V1 and V2"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p><strong>Lead II</strong> shows P waves well (rhythm) and inferior changes; <strong>V5</strong> (or CM5) detects most <strong>lateral</strong> ischaemia. Combined, they detect the majority of intraoperative ischaemic episodes.</p>"},{"id":"pm-img-003","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_G1_6"],"note":"imaging-interpretation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A supine chest radiograph of a ventilated trauma patient shows an unusually deep, lucent costophrenic angle on the left.</p>\n<p>What does this suggest?</p>","options":["Left lower lobe collapse","Left pleural effusion","Left pneumothorax","Pneumoperitoneum","Right main bronchus intubation"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The <strong>deep sulcus sign</strong>: on a <strong>supine</strong> film, air collects anteriorly and inferiorly, deepening the costophrenic angle — a sign of <strong>pneumothorax</strong> that may be missed because the classic apical lung edge is absent.</p>"},{"id":"pm-meas-003","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_1"],"note":"measurement-principles","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A pressure transducer reads consistently 5 mmHg higher than a reference standard across its whole range.</p>\n<p>Which term best describes this error?</p>","options":["Drift of gain","Hysteresis","Imprecision","Non-linearity","Offset (zero) error, a lack of accuracy"],"answer":4,"explain":"<p>A constant difference across the range is an <strong>offset (zero) error</strong> — the device may be <strong>precise</strong> (repeatable) but <strong>inaccurate</strong>. Re-zeroing corrects it. A gain error would increase with the size of the reading.</p>"},{"id":"pm-spo2-003","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_9"],"note":"pulse-oximetry","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient receives an intravenous injection of methylene blue for methaemoglobinaemia.</p>\n<p>What happens to the pulse oximeter reading over the following minutes?</p>","options":["It falls transiently","It is unaffected","It reads exactly 85%","It rises to 100%","It stops displaying a pulse"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Methylene blue absorbs light strongly at around <strong>660 nm</strong>, which the oximeter interprets as deoxyhaemoglobin, so SpO<sub>2</sub> <strong>falls transiently</strong> without true desaturation.</p>"},{"id":"pm-co2-003","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_8"],"note":"capnography","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>After intubation, a capnograph shows a normal square waveform, but the end-tidal CO<sub>2</sub> is 3.0 kPa while the arterial PaCO<sub>2</sub> is 7.5 kPa.</p>\n<p>Which condition best explains the large gradient?</p>","options":["Bronchospasm with a normal-shaped waveform","Hypoventilation","Increased alveolar dead space, for example from low cardiac output","Rebreathing","Sampling line disconnection"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The PaCO<sub>2</sub>–ETCO<sub>2</sub> gap reflects <strong>alveolar dead space</strong> (ventilated but poorly perfused alveoli dilute the expired CO<sub>2</sub>). Low cardiac output, pulmonary embolism, hypovolaemia and high airway pressures widen it.</p>"},{"id":"pm-temp-002","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_3"],"note":"temperature-measurement","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which site gives the best continuous estimate of core temperature during a laparotomy under general anaesthesia?</p>","options":["Axilla","Distal oesophagus","Forehead skin strip","Rectum","Tympanic membrane measured once at the start"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The <strong>distal oesophagus</strong> (lower third, near the heart and great vessels) tracks core temperature closely during anaesthesia. The rectum and bladder lag; skin and axillary sites underestimate.</p>"},{"id":"pm-co-003","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_12"],"note":"cardiac-output-measurement","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What does an oesophageal Doppler monitor measure directly to estimate stroke volume?</p>","options":["Aortic diameter","Blood velocity in the descending aorta over time (stroke distance)","Left ventricular end-diastolic volume","Pulmonary artery temperature change","Thoracic electrical impedance"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The probe measures <strong>blood velocity in the descending thoracic aorta</strong>; the area under the velocity–time curve (<strong>stroke distance</strong>) is multiplied by an estimated aortic cross-sectional area (from a nomogram) and corrected for the fraction of output reaching the descending aorta.</p>"},{"id":"pm-gas-003","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_15"],"note":"gas-analysis","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Why can infrared analysers measure carbon dioxide, nitrous oxide and volatile agents but not oxygen?</p>","options":["Oxygen is paramagnetic","Oxygen is too dilute","Only molecules containing two or more different atoms absorb infrared radiation","Oxygen absorbs ultraviolet light instead","Oxygen reacts with the sample chamber"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Infrared absorption needs a molecule with <strong>two or more dissimilar atoms</strong> (a changing dipole on vibration). O<sub>2</sub> (and N<sub>2</sub>) are diatomic molecules of a single element, so they do not absorb infrared.</p>"},{"id":"pm-bp-003","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_7"],"note":"pressure-measurement","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What is the purpose of the Wheatstone bridge in an invasive pressure transducer?</p>","options":["To convert small changes in strain-gauge resistance into a measurable voltage","To damp the pressure waveform","To earth the patient","To flush the arterial line","To heat the transducer"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>The diaphragm stretches a <strong>strain gauge</strong>, changing its resistance; the <strong>Wheatstone bridge</strong> detects this small change very sensitively as an output voltage, which is amplified and displayed.</p>"},{"id":"pm-nm-003","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_17"],"note":"nm-monitoring","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which muscle's response to nerve stimulation most closely reflects the onset of neuromuscular block at the larynx?</p>","options":["Adductor pollicis","Corrugator supercilii","Flexor hallucis brevis","First dorsal interosseous","Orbicularis oculi"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Corrugator supercilii</strong> (facial nerve stimulation) behaves similarly to the relatively resistant laryngeal and diaphragmatic muscles, so it better reflects intubating conditions. The adductor pollicis is more sensitive and recovers later — it is the standard site for confirming recovery.</p>"},{"id":"pm-pft-003","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_F3_16"],"note":"flow-volume-pft","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Spirometry shows FEV<sub>1</sub> 1.2 L (40% predicted) and FVC 3.0 L (80% predicted).</p>\n<p>What pattern is this?</p>","options":["Mixed restrictive and obstructive","Normal","Obstructive","Restrictive","Upper airway obstruction"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC = 1.2/3.0 = <strong>0.4</strong>, well below 0.7: an <strong>obstructive</strong> pattern (e.g. COPD or asthma). Restrictive patterns reduce both with a normal or high ratio.</p>"},{"id":"pm-img-004","subject":"measurement","codes":["1_GA_M_2"],"note":"imaging-interpretation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Under the Ionising Radiation (Medical Exposure) Regulations, which role is responsible for justifying a medical exposure?</p>","options":["Employer","Operator","Practitioner","Radiation protection supervisor","Referrer"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The <strong>practitioner</strong> justifies each exposure (weighing benefit and risk). The <strong>referrer</strong> provides clinical information requesting it, and the <strong>operator</strong> carries out the practical aspects.</p>"},{"id":"pp-csht-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_37"],"note":"pk-csht-tci","refs":["csht1992"],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient is having total intravenous anaesthesia for a 7-hour operation. The team wants the opioid's offset at the end to be as rapid as after a 1-hour case.</p>\n<p>Which property of remifentanil best explains why it meets this aim?</p>","options":["High lipid solubility","Low pKa","Metabolism by non-specific plasma and tissue esterases","Small volume of distribution","High potency"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Rapid, high-capacity clearance by non-specific esterases keeps remifentanil's context-sensitive half-time at a few minutes <strong>whatever the infusion's duration</strong>.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> High lipid solubility tends to <em>increase</em> peripheral accumulation (as with fentanyl), lengthening offset after long infusions.</li><li><strong>B</strong> A pKa close to physiological pH increases the un-ionised fraction and speeds <em>onset</em>, not offset.</li><li><strong>D</strong> A small volume of distribution helps, but on its own does not make offset independent of duration.</li><li><strong>E</strong> Potency determines dose, not how quickly the effect wears off.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-csht-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_37"],"note":"pk-csht-tci","refs":["csht1992"],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Context-sensitive half-time is defined for a drug given by infusion.</p>\n<p>Which of the following is the best definition?</p>","options":["The time for the effect-site concentration to fall by 50% after a bolus dose","The time for the plasma concentration to fall by 50% after stopping an infusion that has maintained a steady plasma concentration, as a function of infusion duration","The terminal elimination half-life adjusted for the patient's body weight","The time taken for a patient to wake after an infusion is stopped","The time for the plasma concentration to reach 50% of steady state after starting an infusion"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>CSHT is a <strong>plasma</strong> measure after an infusion that <strong>maintained a constant plasma concentration</strong>, and the \"context\" is the infusion's duration.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> refers to the effect site and to a bolus, not an infusion.</li><li><strong>C</strong> The terminal half-life is a different parameter and does not describe offset after infusions of different lengths.</li><li><strong>D</strong> Waking depends on the decrement needed and on other drugs; CSHT does not directly predict it.</li><li><strong>E</strong> describes the approach to steady state after starting an infusion.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-tci-003","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_38"],"note":"pk-csht-tci","refs":["tiva2018"],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 78-year-old woman is anaesthetised with propofol by target-controlled infusion using effect-site targeting. At the same effect-site target, induction is slower than in a younger patient and she becomes hypotensive.</p>\n<p>Which input to the Schnider model is most likely to explain why the pump gives her a smaller initial bolus than it would a younger patient of the same size?</p>","options":["Age","Body mass index","Gender","Height","Lean body mass"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>The Schnider model includes <strong>age</strong>, and age strongly influences its calculated bolus and rates in older patients, so older patients receive less drug for the same effect-site target. Older patients are also more sensitive pharmacodynamically, so lower targets are usually chosen.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>B</strong> BMI is not a direct input to Schnider.</li><li><strong>C</strong>, <strong>D</strong> and <strong>E</strong> Gender, height and lean body mass are used to calculate clearance-related parameters, but for two patients of the same size the difference in the initial bolus here is due to age.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-pk-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_36"],"note":"pk-basics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A drug has a volume of distribution of 70 L and a clearance of 7 L/h.</p>\n<p>What is its elimination half-life?</p>","options":["0.1 h","4.9 h","6.9 h","10 h","14.4 h"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p><strong>t<sub>½</sub> = 0.693 × Vd / CL</strong> = 0.693 × 70 / 7 = <strong>6.9 h</strong>.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>D</strong> (10 h) is the <strong>time constant</strong> (Vd/CL); t<sub>½</sub> = 0.693 × τ.</li><li><strong>E</strong> confuses the relationship (τ = 1.44 × t<sub>½</sub>).</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-pk-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_1"],"note":"pk-basics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A drug is eliminated by first-order kinetics.</p>\n<p>What percentage of the initial plasma concentration remains after three time constants?</p>","options":["5%","12.5%","25%","37%","50%"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>After one time constant 37% remains (e<sup>−1</sup>); after three, e<sup>−3</sup> ≈ <strong>5%</strong>.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>B</strong> (12.5%) is what remains after three <strong>half-lives</strong>.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-pk-003","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_32"],"note":"pk-basics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient in cardiogenic shock with a low cardiac output is given a lidocaine infusion for ventricular arrhythmias.</p>\n<p>Why is the plasma concentration likely to be higher than expected?</p>","options":["Increased protein binding in shock","Increased volume of distribution","Lidocaine is a high-extraction drug, so reduced hepatic blood flow reduces its clearance","Lidocaine is renally excreted unchanged","Shock induces cytochrome P450 enzymes"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Lidocaine has a <strong>high hepatic extraction ratio</strong>, so its clearance depends on <strong>hepatic blood flow</strong>. Low cardiac output reduces hepatic flow and clearance; the central volume of distribution is also reduced, so concentrations rise.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> would lower the free concentration.</li><li><strong>B</strong> would lower concentrations.</li><li><strong>D</strong> Lidocaine is metabolised in the liver.</li><li><strong>E</strong> is incorrect.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-abs-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_30"],"note":"pk-absorption","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>After an oral dose of 100 mg of a drug, the area under the plasma concentration–time curve is 40 mg·h/L. After an intravenous dose of 50 mg, the AUC is 50 mg·h/L.</p>\n<p>What is the oral bioavailability?</p>","options":["20%","40%","50%","80%","100%"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>F = (AUC<sub>oral</sub> / dose<sub>oral</sub>) / (AUC<sub>IV</sub> / dose<sub>IV</sub>)</strong> = (40/100) / (50/50) = 0.4 = <strong>40%</strong>.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>D</strong> forgets to correct for the different doses.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-abs-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_28"],"note":"pk-absorption","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with a 25 µg/h transdermal fentanyl patch develops a fever of 39.5°C and is placed under a forced-air warming blanket.</p>\n<p>What is the main risk?</p>","options":["Decreased fentanyl absorption and withdrawal","Increased fentanyl absorption and respiratory depression","No change, because patch delivery is rate-controlled by the membrane","Patch adhesive failure","Serotonin syndrome"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Heat increases skin blood flow and drug diffusion from the patch and skin depot, <strong>increasing absorption</strong>, which can cause opioid toxicity. Avoid heat sources over patches.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>C</strong> The rate-controlling membrane does not prevent heat-related increases in absorption.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-met-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_33"],"note":"pk-metabolism","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient taking clarithromycin receives a standard dose of midazolam for sedation and remains deeply sedated for many hours.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely mechanism?</p>","options":["Clarithromycin displaces midazolam from albumin","Clarithromycin induces CYP3A4","Clarithromycin inhibits CYP3A4","Clarithromycin inhibits glucuronidation","Clarithromycin reduces renal excretion of midazolam"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Macrolides (clarithromycin, erythromycin) <strong>inhibit CYP3A4</strong>, the main enzyme hydroxylating midazolam, prolonging its effect. Alfentanil and fentanyl are affected similarly.</p>"},{"id":"pp-met-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_39","1_GA_A_8"],"note":"pk-metabolism","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A healthy 25-year-old remains apnoeic 90 minutes after 100 mg of suxamethonium. Her dibucaine number is later measured as 22.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely genotype?</p>","options":["Heterozygous atypical","Homozygous atypical","Homozygous normal with acquired deficiency","Homozygous silent","Homozygous fluoride-resistant"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>A <strong>dibucaine number of about 20</strong> indicates homozygosity for the <strong>atypical (dibucaine-resistant)</strong> gene: the enzyme is poorly inhibited by dibucaine and hydrolyses suxamethonium very slowly. Normal ≈ 80; heterozygotes ≈ 40–60.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>D</strong> Silent-gene homozygotes have almost no enzyme activity; dibucaine number cannot be meaningfully measured.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-pd-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_11","1_GA_G3_12"],"note":"pd-receptors","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>In the presence of a fixed concentration of drug X, the log dose–response curve for an agonist shifts to the right in parallel, with no change in the maximum response.</p>\n<p>Drug X is best described as:</p>","options":["A competitive reversible antagonist","A full agonist at the same receptor","An inverse agonist","An irreversible antagonist","A partial agonist with high efficacy"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>A <strong>competitive reversible antagonist</strong> can be overcome by more agonist, producing a <strong>parallel rightward shift</strong> with the same maximum.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>D</strong> An irreversible (or non-competitive) antagonist reduces the <strong>maximum</strong> response.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-pd-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_24"],"note":"pd-receptors","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Repeated 6 mg boluses of ephedrine produce progressively smaller rises in blood pressure over 20 minutes.</p>\n<p>What is the main mechanism?</p>","options":["Down-regulation of adrenoceptors","Induction of monoamine oxidase","Increased hepatic metabolism","Depletion of noradrenaline stores in sympathetic nerve terminals","Receptor antibody formation"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Ephedrine acts partly <strong>indirectly</strong>, releasing noradrenaline from nerve terminals. Repeated doses deplete these stores faster than they are replenished, causing <strong>tachyphylaxis</strong> within minutes.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> Receptor down-regulation takes hours to days.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-chem-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_8"],"note":"drug-chemistry","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A local anaesthetic has a pKa of 8.4. What proportion is un-ionised at a tissue pH of 7.4?</p>","options":["About 1%","About 9%","About 50%","About 91%","About 99%"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>For a base, log([ionised]/[un-ionised]) = pKa − pH = 1, so the ratio is 10:1. Un-ionised fraction = 1/11 ≈ <strong>9%</strong>.</p>"},{"id":"pp-chem-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_7"],"note":"drug-chemistry","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which property of glycopyrronium explains why it causes less confusion than atropine in elderly patients?</p>","options":["It has a shorter half-life","It is a quaternary ammonium compound that does not cross the blood–brain barrier","It is highly protein-bound","It is metabolised by plasma esterases","It is selective for M<sub>2</sub> receptors"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Glycopyrronium carries a <strong>permanent positive charge</strong> (quaternary amine) and does not cross the blood–brain barrier. Atropine and hyoscine are tertiary amines that enter the CNS.</p>"},{"id":"pp-chem-003","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_10"],"note":"drug-chemistry","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Levobupivacaine is the S(−)-enantiomer of bupivacaine.</p>\n<p>What is the main clinical advantage of using a single enantiomer here?</p>","options":["Faster onset of block","Greater motor block","Longer duration of action","Lower risk of cardiotoxicity","No need for adrenaline"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>The <strong>R-enantiomer</strong> of bupivacaine binds cardiac sodium channels more avidly, so the S-enantiomer (levobupivacaine) is <strong>less cardiotoxic</strong> for similar block characteristics.</p>"},{"id":"pp-inh-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_44","1_GA_G3_42"],"note":"inhalational-uptake","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which factor will slow the rise in alveolar concentration of sevoflurane towards the inspired concentration during inhalational induction?</p>","options":["Decreased cardiac output","High fresh gas flow","High inspired concentration","Increased alveolar ventilation","Increased cardiac output"],"answer":4,"explain":"<p>A <strong>high cardiac output</strong> removes more agent from the alveoli into blood, so F<sub>A</sub>/F<sub>I</sub> rises more slowly and induction is slower.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> Low cardiac output speeds the rise (and may lead to overdose in shocked patients).</li><li><strong>B</strong>, <strong>C</strong> and <strong>D</strong> all speed the rise.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-inh-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_42"],"note":"inhalational-uptake","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which of the following decreases MAC?</p>","options":["Chronic alcohol use","Hyperthermia to 39°C","Hypernatraemia","Infancy (aged 3 months)","Pregnancy"],"answer":4,"explain":"<p><strong>Pregnancy reduces MAC</strong> (by up to about 30–40%), probably through progesterone and endorphins.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong>, <strong>B</strong>, <strong>C</strong> and <strong>D</strong> all <strong>increase</strong> MAC.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-inh-003","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_42","1_GA_F2_16"],"note":"inhalational-agents","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Why does desflurane require a heated, pressurised vaporiser?</p>","options":["It is chemically unstable at room temperature","It is highly soluble in blood","Its boiling point is close to room temperature and its saturated vapour pressure is very high","Its MAC is very low","It reacts with the metal of conventional vaporisers"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Desflurane boils at about <strong>23°C</strong> and has an SVP of about 89 kPa at 20°C, so its output from a variable-bypass vaporiser would be uncontrollable and highly temperature-dependent. The Tec 6-type vaporiser heats it to 39°C (about 2 atmospheres) and injects vapour into the fresh gas.</p>"},{"id":"pp-inh-004","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_42"],"note":"inhalational-agents","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient had vitreoretinal surgery with an intraocular injection of perfluoropropane (C<sub>3</sub>F<sub>8</sub>) gas two weeks ago and now needs an emergency appendicectomy.</p>\n<p>Which anaesthetic drug should be avoided?</p>","options":["Desflurane","Nitrous oxide","Propofol","Rocuronium","Sevoflurane"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Nitrous oxide</strong> is much more soluble than the gas in the bubble and diffuses into it faster than the gas can leave, rapidly <strong>expanding</strong> it and raising intraocular pressure (risking central retinal artery occlusion and blindness). Avoid N<sub>2</sub>O until the bubble has fully resorbed (weeks for C<sub>3</sub>F<sub>8</sub>).</p>"},{"id":"pp-mech-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_43"],"note":"anaesthesia-mechanisms","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which receptor is the principal target of ketamine?</p>","options":["GABA<sub>A</sub> receptor","Glycine receptor","μ-opioid receptor","NMDA receptor","Two-pore domain potassium channel"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Ketamine is a non-competitive <strong>NMDA receptor antagonist</strong> (channel blocker). N<sub>2</sub>O and xenon also act here. Propofol, etomidate and thiopental act mainly at GABA<sub>A</sub> receptors.</p>"},{"id":"pp-iv-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_44"],"note":"iv-induction-agents","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with septic shock needs a rapid sequence induction. The team chooses etomidate for cardiovascular stability.</p>\n<p>What is the main concern specific to etomidate in this patient?</p>","options":["Bronchospasm from histamine release","Emergence hallucinations","Inhibition of adrenal steroid synthesis","Porphyria precipitation","Severe pain on injection"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Even a single dose of etomidate inhibits <strong>11β-hydroxylase</strong>, suppressing cortisol synthesis for many hours. This may matter in sepsis, where adrenal function is important.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>B</strong> is a feature of ketamine.</li><li><strong>D</strong> is associated with barbiturates.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-iv-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_44"],"note":"iv-induction-agents","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What is the main mechanism by which consciousness returns a few minutes after a single induction dose of propofol?</p>","options":["Elimination by the kidneys","Hepatic metabolism","Metabolism in the lungs","Redistribution from the brain to muscle and other tissues","Tolerance at the GABA<sub>A</sub> receptor"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>After a single bolus, the brain concentration falls because drug <strong>redistributes</strong> from the vessel-rich group (brain) to muscle and then fat. Metabolism (which for propofol is rapid and partly extrahepatic) determines recovery after infusions.</p>"},{"id":"pp-bdz-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_45"],"note":"benzodiazepines-sedation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>How do benzodiazepines enhance GABA<sub>A</sub> receptor function?</p>","options":["By blocking chloride channels","By directly opening the chloride channel without GABA","By increasing the duration of chloride channel opening","By increasing the frequency of chloride channel opening in the presence of GABA","By inhibiting GABA reuptake"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Benzodiazepines are positive allosteric modulators that increase the <strong>frequency</strong> of channel opening in response to GABA. Barbiturates increase the <strong>duration</strong> of opening and at high concentrations can open the channel directly.</p>"},{"id":"pp-bdz-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_PS_F_1","1_PS_D_1"],"note":"benzodiazepines-sedation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During endoscopy under sedation, a patient responds purposefully only to repeated painful stimulation and needs a jaw thrust to maintain the airway.</p>\n<p>What level of sedation has been reached?</p>","options":["Minimal sedation","Moderate (conscious) sedation","Deep sedation","General anaesthesia","Dissociative sedation"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>A purposeful response only to repeated or painful stimulation, with possible need for airway support, is <strong>deep sedation</strong>. Moderate sedation requires a purposeful response to voice or light touch with no airway intervention.</p>"},{"id":"pp-op-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_50"],"note":"opioids","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Why does alfentanil have a faster onset than fentanyl despite being less lipid-soluble?</p>","options":["It has a higher pKa","It has a larger volume of distribution","It is metabolised to an active compound","Most of the drug is un-ionised at physiological pH because of its low pKa","It is more potent"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Alfentanil's <strong>pKa is about 6.5</strong>, so about <strong>90% is un-ionised</strong> at pH 7.4 and diffuses rapidly across the blood–brain barrier. Fentanyl (pKa ~8.4) is less than 10% un-ionised. Alfentanil's small Vd also means a high concentration gradient.</p>"},{"id":"pp-op-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_50","1_GA_G3_39"],"note":"opioids","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A child is given codeine after tonsillectomy and becomes profoundly drowsy with a respiratory rate of 6.</p>\n<p>Which genetic variation is most likely responsible?</p>","options":["CYP2D6 poor metaboliser","CYP2D6 ultra-rapid metaboliser","CYP3A4 deficiency","Plasma cholinesterase deficiency","Slow acetylator status"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Codeine is a pro-drug converted to <strong>morphine</strong> by <strong>CYP2D6</strong>. <strong>Ultra-rapid metabolisers</strong> produce high morphine levels, causing respiratory depression; deaths in children after tonsillectomy led to codeine being contraindicated for analgesia in this setting in under-18s.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> Poor metabolisers get little analgesia, not toxicity.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-op-003","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_50"],"note":"opioids","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient given morphine in recovery is reversed with naloxone 200 µg IV and wakes. Forty-five minutes later on the ward he is found unresponsive with a respiratory rate of 5.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely explanation?</p>","options":["Anaphylaxis to naloxone","Naloxone has a shorter duration of action than morphine","Naloxone is a partial agonist","Naloxone was given into a subcutaneous tissue","Opioid withdrawal"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Naloxone's effect lasts about <strong>30–60 minutes</strong>, shorter than morphine's, so <strong>re-narcotisation</strong> occurs. Patients need observation, and repeated doses or an infusion may be needed.</p>"},{"id":"pp-nsaid-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_48"],"note":"paracetamol-nsaids","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What is the mechanism of liver injury in paracetamol overdose?</p>","options":["Direct inhibition of hepatic COX-2","Excess glucuronidation producing a toxic conjugate","Immune-mediated hepatitis","Saturation of conjugation pathways, with the metabolite NAPQI exceeding glutathione capacity","Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation by salicylate"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>In overdose, glucuronidation and sulphation saturate, more paracetamol is oxidised (CYP2E1) to <strong>NAPQI</strong>, and glutathione is depleted, so NAPQI binds hepatocyte proteins and causes necrosis. <strong>Acetylcysteine</strong> replenishes glutathione.</p>"},{"id":"pp-nsaid-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_49"],"note":"paracetamol-nsaids","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 70-year-old taking ramipril and furosemide develops acute kidney injury after two days of regular ibuprofen following a hip replacement.</p>\n<p>Which mechanism best explains the renal effect of the NSAID?</p>","options":["Direct tubular toxicity","Glomerulonephritis","Increased renin secretion","Inhibition of prostaglandin-mediated afferent arteriolar vasodilatation","Obstruction of the renal tubules by crystals"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>When renal perfusion is threatened, <strong>prostaglandins dilate the afferent arteriole</strong> to preserve GFR. NSAIDs remove this; ACE inhibitors remove angiotensin II efferent constriction and diuretics reduce volume (the \"triple whammy\").</p>"},{"id":"pp-la-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_46"],"note":"local-anaesthetics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which property of a local anaesthetic most strongly determines its duration of action?</p>","options":["Degree of ionisation at physiological pH","Ester or amide linkage","Lipid solubility","Molecular weight","Protein binding"],"answer":4,"explain":"<p><strong>Protein binding</strong> (to sodium channel proteins as well as plasma proteins) correlates best with <strong>duration</strong>: bupivacaine (~95%) lasts longer than lidocaine (~65%).</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> (pKa) determines speed of onset.</li><li><strong>C</strong> determines potency.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-la-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_46","1_RA_K_1"],"note":"local-anaesthetics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 50 kg woman is to have an ankle block with plain levobupivacaine 0.5%. Using a maximum dose of 2 mg/kg, what is the largest volume that may be given?</p>","options":["10 mL","15 mL","20 mL","25 mL","30 mL"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>0.5% = 5 mg/mL. Maximum dose = 2 × 50 = 100 mg → 100 / 5 = <strong>20 mL</strong>. (Remember to include any other local anaesthetic given, and that maximum doses are only a guide.)</p>"},{"id":"pp-sux-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_51"],"note":"nmb-suxamethonium","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>After a prolonged infusion of suxamethonium, the train-of-four shows fade and a post-tetanic count response is seen.</p>\n<p>What does this indicate?</p>","options":["Anticholinesterase overdose","Malignant hyperthermia","Phase I block","Phase II block","Residual non-depolarising block from a previous drug"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p><strong>Phase II block</strong> develops with large or repeated doses and shows the features of a non-depolarising block: <strong>fade</strong> on TOF and tetanus, and <strong>post-tetanic facilitation</strong>. Phase I (depolarising) block shows no fade.</p>"},{"id":"pp-sux-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_51","1_GA_A_8"],"note":"nmb-suxamethonium","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>In which patient is suxamethonium most likely to cause life-threatening hyperkalaemia?</p>","options":["A patient 2 hours after a 30% burn","A patient 3 weeks after a spinal cord injury with paraplegia","A patient with chronic kidney disease and a potassium of 5.0 mmol/L","A patient with myasthenia gravis","A pregnant woman at term"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Denervation</strong> (spinal cord injury, stroke) causes proliferation of <strong>extrajunctional receptors</strong> from about 48–72 hours; suxamethonium then causes massive K<sup>+</sup> efflux, with risk that persists for months.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> Risk starts about 24–48 h after a burn, not at 2 hours.</li><li><strong>C</strong> A normal-high K<sup>+</sup> in stable renal failure rises by only about 0.5 mmol/L.</li><li><strong>D</strong> Myasthenics are resistant to suxamethonium.</li><li><strong>E</strong> Pregnancy slightly reduces cholinesterase but does not cause hyperkalaemia.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-ndp-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_52"],"note":"nmb-nondepolarising","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which non-depolarising neuromuscular blocker is most suitable for a patient with both renal and hepatic failure?</p>","options":["Cisatracurium","Mivacurium","Pancuronium","Rocuronium","Vecuronium"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p><strong>Cisatracurium</strong> is eliminated mainly by <strong>Hofmann degradation</strong> (organ-independent), so its duration is predictable in renal and hepatic failure and it releases little histamine.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>B</strong> Mivacurium depends on plasma cholinesterase, which is reduced in liver failure.</li><li><strong>C</strong>, <strong>D</strong> and <strong>E</strong> depend on hepatic and/or renal elimination.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-ndp-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_52"],"note":"nmb-nondepolarising","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Rocuronium produces intubating conditions faster than vecuronium at equipotent doses.</p>\n<p>Which principle best explains this?</p>","options":["Rocuronium is more lipid-soluble","Rocuronium is more potent, so fewer molecules are needed","Rocuronium is less potent, so more molecules are given and reach the junction faster","Rocuronium is not bound to plasma proteins","Rocuronium undergoes Hofmann degradation"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The <strong>Bowman principle</strong>: onset is inversely related to potency. A less potent drug is given in a larger molar dose, creating a steeper concentration gradient to the neuromuscular junction.</p>"},{"id":"pp-rev-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_53"],"note":"nmb-reversal","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient given rocuronium 1.2 mg/kg for a rapid sequence induction cannot be intubated or oxygenated, and the team decides to reverse the block immediately.</p>\n<p>Which statement is correct?</p>","options":["Neostigmine 5 mg will reverse the block within 2 minutes","Sugammadex at the high \"immediate reversal\" dose can reverse this block within minutes","Sugammadex is ineffective against high-dose rocuronium","Reversal of neuromuscular block guarantees a patent airway","Flumazenil should be given with sugammadex"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Sugammadex</strong> encapsulates rocuronium and at the high immediate-reversal dose (per product information) restores neuromuscular function within a few minutes. However, reversal may not relieve an airway obstructed for other reasons, and <strong>front-of-neck access must not be delayed</strong>.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> Neostigmine cannot reverse deep block (ceiling effect).</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-rev-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_F3_17","1_GA_D_4"],"note":"nmb-reversal","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What is the minimum train-of-four ratio at the adductor pollicis that is generally accepted as adequate recovery before tracheal extubation?</p>","options":["0.4","0.5","0.7","0.9","1.2"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>A <strong>TOF ratio ≥ 0.9</strong> (measured quantitatively) is the accepted threshold: below it, pharyngeal function and airway protection may be impaired. Fade cannot reliably be detected by touch or sight once the ratio exceeds about 0.4.</p>"},{"id":"pp-ach-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_57"],"note":"anticholinergics-cholinergics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 12-year-old becomes agitated, flushed, hot and confused in recovery after receiving hyoscine hydrobromide. The pupils are dilated and the skin is dry.</p>\n<p>What is the specific treatment if symptoms are severe?</p>","options":["Atropine","Dantrolene","Glycopyrronium","Neostigmine","Physostigmine"],"answer":4,"explain":"<p>This is the <strong>central anticholinergic syndrome</strong>. <strong>Physostigmine</strong> is a tertiary anticholinesterase that crosses the blood–brain barrier. Neostigmine is quaternary and does not.</p>"},{"id":"pp-sym-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_59"],"note":"sympathomimetics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient on long-term β-blockers with severe heart failure has a low cardiac output state after surgery.</p>\n<p>Which inotrope works independently of β-adrenoceptors?</p>","options":["Adrenaline","Dobutamine","Dopamine","Isoprenaline","Milrinone"],"answer":4,"explain":"<p><strong>Milrinone</strong> inhibits <strong>phosphodiesterase III</strong>, raising cAMP without needing β-receptor stimulation. It is an <strong>inodilator</strong> (inotropy with vasodilation), useful when β-receptors are blocked or downregulated.</p>"},{"id":"pp-sym-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_56"],"note":"sympathomimetics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient taking phenelzine develops hypotension under anaesthesia.</p>\n<p>Which vasopressor is most likely to cause a hypertensive crisis?</p>","options":["Adrenaline in small doses","Ephedrine","Noradrenaline in small doses","Phenylephrine in small doses","Vasopressin"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>MAOIs increase noradrenaline stores in nerve terminals. <strong>Indirectly acting</strong> sympathomimetics such as <strong>ephedrine</strong> release these stores, causing an exaggerated, dangerous hypertensive response. Direct-acting agents in reduced, titrated doses are preferred.</p>"},{"id":"pp-htn-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_62"],"note":"antihypertensives","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient receives a sodium nitroprusside infusion at high doses for 24 hours and develops a metabolic acidosis with a rising lactate and a high mixed venous oxygen saturation.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely cause, and its specific treatment?</p>","options":["Cyanide toxicity; hydroxocobalamin or sodium thiosulfate","Methaemoglobinaemia; methylene blue","Propofol infusion syndrome; stop propofol","Rebound hypertension; labetalol","Thiocyanate toxicity; haemodialysis alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Nitroprusside releases <strong>cyanide</strong>, which blocks cytochrome oxidase: cells cannot use oxygen, so lactate rises and mixed venous saturation is high. Treat with <strong>hydroxocobalamin</strong> (forms cyanocobalamin) and/or <strong>sodium thiosulfate</strong>; stop the infusion.</p>"},{"id":"pp-arr-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_61"],"note":"antiarrhythmics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with a regular narrow-complex tachycardia at 180/min is given adenosine 6 mg IV without effect.</p>\n<p>Which interaction would most increase the effect and duration of adenosine?</p>","options":["Aminophylline","Caffeine","Dipyridamole","Theophylline","Verapamil"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p><strong>Dipyridamole blocks adenosine reuptake</strong>, greatly potentiating it: use a much smaller dose. Methylxanthines (aminophylline, theophylline, caffeine) <strong>antagonise</strong> adenosine receptors.</p>"},{"id":"pp-arr-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_61"],"note":"antiarrhythmics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which electrolyte abnormality most increases the risk of digoxin toxicity?</p>","options":["Hyperkalaemia","Hypermagnesaemia","Hypernatraemia","Hypocalcaemia","Hypokalaemia"],"answer":4,"explain":"<p>Digoxin competes with K<sup>+</sup> for the Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase. <strong>Hypokalaemia</strong> increases digoxin binding and toxicity (so do hypomagnesaemia and hypercalcaemia).</p>"},{"id":"pp-card-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_60"],"note":"cardiac-drugs","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which antianginal drug slows the heart rate by blocking the funny current (I<sub>f</sub>) in the sinoatrial node, without negative inotropic effect?</p>","options":["Diltiazem","Ivabradine","Nicorandil","Ranolazine","Verapamil"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Ivabradine</strong> blocks HCN channels carrying I<sub>f</sub>, slowing phase 4 depolarisation in sinus rhythm. It does not reduce contractility.</p>"},{"id":"pp-card-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_60","1_POM_C_3"],"note":"cardiac-drugs","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient taking dapagliflozin for heart failure has emergency surgery and afterwards is breathless and vomiting. Glucose is 9 mmol/L, pH 7.18, bicarbonate 10 mmol/L and blood ketones 5.2 mmol/L.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>","options":["Euglycaemic diabetic ketoacidosis","Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state","Lactic acidosis from metformin","Renal tubular acidosis","Starvation ketosis without acidosis"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p><strong>SGLT2 inhibitors</strong> can cause <strong>euglycaemic DKA</strong>, especially with fasting, surgery and acute illness: ketoacidosis with near-normal glucose. Treat as DKA (fluids, insulin with glucose, potassium) and stop the drug.</p>"},{"id":"pp-ac-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_63"],"note":"anticoagulants","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient on dabigatran needs emergency laparotomy for a perforation and is bleeding.</p>\n<p>What is the specific reversal agent?</p>","options":["Andexanet alfa","Idarucizumab","Protamine","Tranexamic acid","Vitamin K"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Idarucizumab</strong> is a monoclonal antibody fragment that binds dabigatran (a direct thrombin inhibitor).</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> reverses factor Xa inhibitors (apixaban, rivaroxaban).</li><li><strong>C</strong> reverses heparin.</li><li><strong>E</strong> reverses warfarin (slowly).</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-ac-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_63","1_POM_L_3"],"note":"anticoagulants","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient receives prophylactic-dose enoxaparin at 18:00. Assuming normal renal function, when is the earliest time that a spinal anaesthetic would usually be considered appropriate according to UK guidance?</p>","options":["20:00 the same day","00:00","06:00 the next morning","18:00 the next day","48 hours later"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>UK guidance recommends waiting about <strong>12 hours after a prophylactic dose</strong> of LMWH (24 hours after a treatment dose) before neuraxial block. Always check the current guideline and consider renal function.</p>"},{"id":"pp-proc-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_64"],"note":"procoagulants-blood-products","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What is the mechanism of action of tranexamic acid?</p>","options":["Activation of factor VII","Binding to lysine sites on plasminogen, preventing its binding to fibrin","Direct inhibition of thrombin","Release of von Willebrand factor from endothelium","Replacement of fibrinogen"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Tranexamic acid is a <strong>lysine analogue</strong> that blocks the lysine-binding sites on plasminogen, preventing fibrin binding and <strong>fibrinolysis</strong>. In trauma it should be given within 3 hours of injury.</p>"},{"id":"pp-fl-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_66"],"note":"iv-fluids","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient receives 6 L of 0.9% sodium chloride during resuscitation.</p>\n<p>Which acid–base disturbance is most likely?</p>","options":["Hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis with a normal anion gap","Lactic acidosis with a raised anion gap","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","No acid–base change"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>0.9% saline contains 154 mmol/L of chloride (much more than plasma). Large volumes cause a <strong>hyperchloraemic, normal anion gap metabolic acidosis</strong> (in Stewart terms, the strong ion difference falls).</p>"},{"id":"pp-ae-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_72"],"note":"antiemetics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 22-year-old woman develops torticollis and oculogyric crisis after an antiemetic given in recovery.</p>\n<p>Which drug is most likely responsible?</p>","options":["Cyclizine","Dexamethasone","Metoclopramide","Ondansetron","Aprepitant"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p><strong>D<sub>2</sub> antagonists</strong> such as metoclopramide (and droperidol, haloperidol) can cause acute <strong>dystonic reactions</strong>, especially in young women. Treat with an anticholinergic such as procyclidine.</p>"},{"id":"pp-ae-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_D_3"],"note":"antiemetics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient given ondansetron and dexamethasone at induction has severe nausea in recovery 30 minutes after the end of surgery.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate rescue antiemetic?</p>","options":["A second dose of dexamethasone","A second dose of ondansetron","A drug from a different class, such as cyclizine","No further antiemetic until 6 hours have passed","Propofol infusion at anaesthetic doses"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The consensus guidelines recommend rescue with an antiemetic from a <strong>different class</strong> from those already given; repeating the same drug within about 6 hours adds little.</p>"},{"id":"pp-gi-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_71","1_GA_A_12"],"note":"gi-drugs","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Why is sodium citrate preferred to magnesium trisilicate before general anaesthesia for emergency caesarean section?</p>","options":["It also increases gastric emptying","It is non-particulate, so causes less lung damage if aspirated","It lasts for 6 hours","It reduces gastric acid secretion","It tightens the lower oesophageal sphincter"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Particulate antacids</strong> cause severe pneumonitis if aspirated. <strong>Sodium citrate</strong> is a clear, non-particulate antacid that neutralises acid already present; its effect is short (about 20–30 minutes).</p>"},{"id":"pp-resp-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_68"],"note":"respiratory-drugs","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient on regular theophylline is started on ciprofloxacin and develops nausea, tachycardia and a seizure.</p>\n<p>What is the mechanism?</p>","options":["Additive seizure threshold lowering only","Ciprofloxacin induces CYP1A2","Ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2, increasing theophylline levels","Displacement of theophylline from albumin","Reduced renal excretion of theophylline"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Theophylline is metabolised by <strong>CYP1A2</strong>; ciprofloxacin (and erythromycin) <strong>inhibit</strong> it, raising levels into the toxic range (narrow therapeutic index). Smoking induces CYP1A2 and lowers levels.</p>"},{"id":"pp-diur-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_73"],"note":"diuretics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which diuretic is most likely to cause hypercalcaemia?</p>","options":["Acetazolamide","Bendroflumethiazide","Furosemide","Mannitol","Spironolactone"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Thiazides</strong> increase distal calcium reabsorption and can cause <strong>hypercalcaemia</strong> (and are used to reduce calcium stone formation). <strong>Loop diuretics</strong> increase calcium excretion and can treat hypercalcaemia.</p>"},{"id":"pp-cns-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_75","1_GA_G3_21"],"note":"cns-drugs","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient taking sertraline receives tramadol after surgery and develops agitation, tremor, hyperreflexia, inducible clonus and a temperature of 38.6°C.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>","options":["Anticholinergic syndrome","Malignant hyperthermia","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Opioid withdrawal","Serotonin syndrome"],"answer":4,"explain":"<p><strong>Serotonin syndrome</strong> (SSRI plus tramadol's serotonin reuptake inhibition): neuromuscular hyperactivity (<strong>clonus, hyperreflexia</strong>), autonomic instability and altered mental state, developing within hours.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>C</strong> NMS develops over days, with <strong>rigidity</strong> (lead-pipe) and bradyreflexia, after dopamine antagonists.</li><li><strong>B</strong> MH occurs with triggers during anaesthesia.</li></ul>"},{"id":"pp-cns-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_74"],"note":"cns-drugs","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with Parkinson's disease is nauseated after surgery.</p>\n<p>Which antiemetic is most appropriate?</p>","options":["Droperidol","Haloperidol","Metoclopramide","Ondansetron","Prochlorperazine"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Central <strong>dopamine antagonists</strong> worsen Parkinsonism and should be avoided. <strong>Ondansetron</strong> (5-HT<sub>3</sub> antagonist) or domperidone (peripheral D<sub>2</sub> antagonist) are appropriate.</p>"},{"id":"pp-dm-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_76"],"note":"diabetes-drugs","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with type 1 diabetes is nil by mouth for a morning operation. Her usual regimen is glargine at night and aspart with meals.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate plan for her glargine the night before surgery?</p>","options":["Omit it completely","Give about 80% of the usual dose","Double the dose","Give it only if the morning glucose is high","Replace it with oral gliclazide"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Basal insulin must never be stopped</strong> in type 1 diabetes (ketoacidosis). Guidance is to continue the long-acting insulin, usually at about <strong>80%</strong> of the dose, while mealtime insulin is omitted when not eating.</p>"},{"id":"pp-ster-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_77"],"note":"steroids-thyroid-drugs","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which corticosteroid has the greatest glucocorticoid potency with negligible mineralocorticoid activity?</p>","options":["Dexamethasone","Fludrocortisone","Hydrocortisone","Methylprednisolone","Prednisolone"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p><strong>Dexamethasone</strong> is about 25–30 times as potent a glucocorticoid as hydrocortisone, with virtually no mineralocorticoid effect. Fludrocortisone is mainly mineralocorticoid.</p>"},{"id":"pp-abx-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_81"],"note":"antimicrobials","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which antibiotic is most likely to prolong the action of rocuronium?</p>","options":["Amoxicillin","Cefuroxime","Gentamicin","Metronidazole","Vancomycin"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p><strong>Aminoglycosides</strong> reduce prejunctional ACh release (Ca<sup>2+</sup> antagonism) and have postjunctional effects, <strong>potentiating non-depolarising block</strong>. Clindamycin and polymyxins also do this.</p>"},{"id":"pp-abx-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_POM_J_6"],"note":"antimicrobials","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>When should intravenous antibiotic prophylaxis ideally be given for a clean-contaminated operation?</p>","options":["At the end of surgery","Within 60 minutes before the skin incision","The evening before surgery","Only if the operation lasts over 4 hours","Three hours before incision"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Give prophylaxis <strong>within 60 minutes before incision</strong> so that tissue concentrations are adequate when contamination occurs; re-dose for long procedures or major blood loss.</p>"},{"id":"pp-mh-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_E_25"],"note":"mh-dantrolene","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which is the earliest and most specific sign of malignant hyperthermia during a volatile anaesthetic with controlled ventilation?</p>","options":["Hyperkalaemia","Myoglobinuria","Temperature above 40°C","Unexplained rise in end-tidal CO<sub>2</sub> despite increased minute ventilation","Ventricular arrhythmias"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Hypermetabolism produces large amounts of CO<sub>2</sub> early, so an <strong>unexplained rising ETCO<sub>2</sub></strong> (with tachycardia) is typically the first sign. Hyperthermia is often a <strong>late</strong> sign.</p>"},{"id":"pp-mh-002","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_E_25"],"note":"mh-dantrolene","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What is the mechanism of action of dantrolene?</p>","options":["Block of acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction","Block of L-type calcium channels in cardiac muscle","Inhibition of calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum via the ryanodine receptor","Inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis in the hypothalamus","Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Dantrolene binds the <strong>ryanodine receptor (RyR1)</strong> and reduces <strong>Ca<sup>2+</sup> release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum</strong> in skeletal muscle. It does not act at the neuromuscular junction.</p>"},{"id":"pp-int-001","subject":"pharmacology","codes":["1_GA_G3_25"],"note":"drug-interactions","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>On an isobologram of doses of propofol and midazolam producing loss of consciousness, the line of combined doses bows towards the origin.</p>\n<p>What does this indicate?</p>","options":["Additive interaction","Antagonism","No interaction","Pharmaceutical incompatibility","Synergy"],"answer":4,"explain":"<p>If lower doses than expected from simple addition produce the same effect, the isobole bows <strong>towards the origin</strong>: <strong>synergy</strong>. A straight line is additive; bowing away is antagonism.</p>"},{"id":"px-si-001","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F2_2"],"note":"si-units-mechanics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A pressure of 20 cmH<sub>2</sub>O is approximately equal to which of the following?</p>","options":["0.2 kPa","2 kPa","15 kPa","20 mmHg","27 mmHg"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>1 kPa ≈ 10.2 cmH<sub>2</sub>O, so 20 cmH<sub>2</sub>O ≈ <strong>2 kPa</strong> (≈ 15 mmHg).</p>\n<ul><li><strong>D</strong> and <strong>E</strong> confuse cmH<sub>2</sub>O with mmHg (1 mmHg ≈ 1.36 cmH<sub>2</sub>O).</li></ul>"},{"id":"px-si-002","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F2_11","1_GA_F3_11"],"note":"si-units-mechanics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>An arterial pressure transducer is accidentally positioned 20 cm below the level of the patient's heart.</p>\n<p>What is the approximate error in the displayed mean arterial pressure?</p>","options":["2 mmHg too low","7.5 mmHg too high","15 mmHg too high","15 mmHg too low","20 mmHg too high"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>A column of fluid exerts <strong>ρgh</strong>: 10 cm of water ≈ 7.4 mmHg. A transducer 20 cm below the heart reads about <strong>15 mmHg too high</strong> (the fluid column adds pressure).</p>"},{"id":"px-gas-001","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F2_8","1_GA_F1_7"],"note":"gas-laws","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A size E oxygen cylinder (nominal capacity 680 L at a full gauge pressure of 137 bar) reads 45 bar. The patient is receiving 10 L/min.</p>\n<p>Approximately how long will the cylinder last?</p>","options":["7 minutes","22 minutes","45 minutes","68 minutes","137 minutes"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>By <strong>Boyle's law</strong>, contents are proportional to pressure: 680 × 45/137 ≈ 223 L. At 10 L/min this lasts about <strong>22 minutes</strong>.</p>"},{"id":"px-gas-002","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F2_9","1_GA_F1_4"],"note":"gas-laws","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>The pressure gauge on a nitrous oxide cylinder at room temperature reads 52 bar.</p>\n<p>Which statement is correct?</p>","options":["The cylinder is about one-third full","The cylinder is full, because 52 bar is the filling pressure of gaseous N<sub>2</sub>O","The reading gives no indication of contents until almost all the liquid has vaporised","The reading will rise as N<sub>2</sub>O is used","Nitrous oxide is above its critical temperature in the cylinder"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>N<sub>2</sub>O's critical temperature (36.5°C) is above room temperature, so the cylinder contains <strong>liquid in equilibrium with vapour</strong>. The gauge shows the <strong>saturated vapour pressure</strong>, which stays constant (at constant temperature) until the liquid is exhausted. Contents are judged by <strong>weight</strong>.</p>"},{"id":"px-gas-003","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F2_8"],"note":"gas-laws","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which gas law best explains why a larger volume of oxygen dissolves in plasma when a patient breathes 100% oxygen in a hyperbaric chamber at 3 atmospheres absolute?</p>","options":["Boyle's law","Charles' law","Dalton's law","Graham's law","Henry's law"],"answer":4,"explain":"<p><strong>Henry's law:</strong> at a given temperature, the amount of gas dissolved in a liquid is proportional to its <strong>partial pressure</strong> above the liquid.</p>"},{"id":"px-vap-001","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F2_16"],"note":"vapours-humidity","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What determines the saturated vapour pressure of a volatile anaesthetic in a vaporising chamber?</p>","options":["Ambient pressure","Fresh gas flow rate","Temperature of the liquid","The concentration dial setting","The volume of liquid in the chamber"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>SVP depends <strong>only on the agent and its temperature</strong>. Ambient pressure, flow and the volume of liquid do not change it. (Cooling from latent heat lowers SVP, hence temperature compensation.)</p>"},{"id":"px-vap-002","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F2_5"],"note":"vapours-humidity","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Gas saturated with water vapour at 21°C (about 18 g/m<sup>3</sup>) is warmed to 37°C without adding water.</p>\n<p>What happens to its absolute and relative humidity?</p>","options":["Both fall","Both rise","Absolute humidity is unchanged; relative humidity falls","Absolute humidity rises; relative humidity is unchanged","Absolute humidity falls; relative humidity rises"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The mass of water per volume (<strong>absolute</strong> humidity) is unchanged, but warmer gas can hold more water (about 44 g/m<sup>3</sup> at 37°C), so <strong>relative</strong> humidity falls to about 40%. The airway must add water to reach saturation.</p>"},{"id":"px-vp-001","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F1_8"],"note":"vaporisers","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>In a variable-bypass vaporiser, what is the function of the bimetallic strip?</p>","options":["To prevent two vaporisers being used at once","To reduce the pumping effect","To adjust the splitting ratio as the liquid cools, sending more gas through the vaporising chamber","To measure the level of liquid agent","To warm the liquid agent"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>As agent vaporises, the liquid cools and its SVP falls. The <strong>bimetallic strip</strong> (temperature compensation) narrows the bypass as temperature falls, sending more gas through the chamber so output remains constant.</p>"},{"id":"px-vp-002","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F1_8"],"note":"vaporisers","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A desflurane Tec 6-type vaporiser is used in a hospital at high altitude (barometric pressure 70 kPa) with the dial set to 6%.</p>\n<p>What is the effect compared with sea level?</p>","options":["Delivered partial pressure is higher","Delivered partial pressure is lower, so the dial must be turned up","Delivered partial pressure is unchanged","The vaporiser cannot be used above sea level","The percentage output falls"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The Tec 6 delivers a set <strong>percentage</strong> (volume fraction) regardless of ambient pressure. At 70 kPa, 6% gives a partial pressure of about 4.2 kPa rather than about 6 kPa, so the <strong>effect is less</strong>: the dial must be increased (by the ratio 101/70). Variable-bypass vaporisers behave differently (their partial pressure output stays about constant).</p>"},{"id":"px-flow-001","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F2_12"],"note":"flow-physics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>The internal radius of a cannula is doubled while length, viscosity and pressure are unchanged, and flow remains laminar.</p>\n<p>By what factor does flow increase?</p>","options":["2","4","8","16","32"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Hagen–Poiseuille: flow ∝ <strong>r<sup>4</sup></strong>, so doubling the radius increases flow <strong>2<sup>4</sup> = 16-fold</strong>.</p>"},{"id":"px-flow-002","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F2_13"],"note":"flow-physics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with stridor from a laryngeal tumour is given heliox (79% helium, 21% oxygen) while awaiting definitive airway management.</p>\n<p>Why does heliox reduce the work of breathing?</p>","options":["Helium dilates the bronchi","Helium has a lower density, reducing resistance where flow is turbulent","Helium has a lower viscosity, reducing resistance where flow is laminar","Helium improves oxygen diffusion across the alveolar membrane","Helium increases the oxygen content of blood"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Flow through a tight upper airway stenosis is <strong>turbulent</strong>, and turbulent flow depends on gas <strong>density</strong>. Helium's low density reduces the Reynolds number and resistance. Its viscosity is similar to oxygen's, so it offers little benefit for laminar flow.</p>"},{"id":"px-flow-003","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F2_15","1_GA_C_2"],"note":"flow-physics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient breathing 60 breaths per minute with a peak inspiratory flow of 80 L/min is given oxygen through a 40% Venturi mask supplied with 15 L/min of oxygen (air entrainment ratio about 1:3).</p>\n<p>What is the most likely effect?</p>","options":["FiO<sub>2</sub> will be exactly 40%","FiO<sub>2</sub> will be higher than 40%","FiO<sub>2</sub> will be lower than 40% because inspiratory flow exceeds total mask flow","The mask will deliver 100% oxygen","The Venturi device stops entraining air at high respiratory rates"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Total flow from the device = 15 L/min O<sub>2</sub> + about 45 L/min entrained air = about 60 L/min, which is <strong>less than the peak inspiratory flow</strong> (80 L/min). The patient draws in extra room air around the mask, <strong>diluting</strong> the FiO<sub>2</sub> below 40%. Fixed-performance devices only work when total flow exceeds peak inspiratory flow.</p>"},{"id":"px-sup-001","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F1_7"],"note":"gas-supply","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What is the principle of the Bourdon gauge used on gas cylinders?</p>","options":["A column of mercury rises with pressure","A coiled tube of oval cross-section straightens as internal pressure rises","A piezoelectric crystal generates a voltage proportional to pressure","A strain gauge in a Wheatstone bridge changes resistance","A thermistor changes resistance with gas temperature"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The <strong>Bourdon gauge</strong> uses a flattened, coiled tube that tends to straighten as pressure inside it rises, driving a pointer through a mechanism. It measures <strong>gauge</strong> pressure and needs no power.</p>"},{"id":"px-sup-002","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F1_4"],"note":"gas-supply","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What is the usual pipeline pressure of oxygen in UK hospitals?</p>","options":["1 bar","4 bar","7 bar","44 bar","137 bar"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Medical oxygen, air and N<sub>2</sub>O are delivered by pipeline at about <strong>4 bar (400 kPa) gauge</strong>. Surgical air for power tools is at about 7 bar. Full oxygen cylinders are about 137 bar.</p>"},{"id":"px-mach-001","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F1_1"],"note":"anaesthetic-machine","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>On a traditional anaesthetic machine, why is the oxygen flowmeter placed downstream of the other flowmeters on the back bar?</p>","options":["To allow the oxygen flush to bypass the vaporisers","So that a leak in an upstream flowmeter tube leaks other gases rather than oxygen","To prevent the pumping effect","Because oxygen has the highest density","To reduce resistance to oxygen flow"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>With oxygen added <strong>last</strong>, a crack in an upstream (e.g. N<sub>2</sub>O or air) tube leaks mainly that gas, reducing the risk of delivering a hypoxic mixture.</p>"},{"id":"px-mach-002","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F1_1"],"note":"anaesthetic-machine","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which safety feature prevents the delivery of a mixture of nitrous oxide and oxygen containing less than about 25% oxygen from the flowmeters?</p>","options":["Anti-hypoxic (hypoxic guard) linkage","Non-return valves","Oxygen failure warning device","Pin index system","Pressure relief valve"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>The <strong>hypoxic guard</strong> mechanically (chains or gears) or electronically links N<sub>2</sub>O and O<sub>2</sub> flow so that the oxygen proportion cannot fall below about 25%. It does not protect against other hypoxic gas mixtures or pipeline errors, so an oxygen analyser is still essential.</p>"},{"id":"px-bs-001","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F1_18"],"note":"breathing-systems","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which breathing system is most efficient (needs the lowest fresh gas flow to prevent rebreathing) for a spontaneously breathing adult?</p>","options":["Mapleson A (Magill or Lack)","Mapleson B","Mapleson C","Mapleson D (Bain)","Mapleson E"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>In spontaneous ventilation the <strong>Mapleson A</strong> needs a fresh gas flow only about equal to <strong>alveolar minute ventilation</strong> (about 70–80 mL/kg/min) because dead space gas is retained and alveolar gas is vented through the APL valve near the patient. It is inefficient for controlled ventilation, where the Mapleson D is best.</p>"},{"id":"px-bs-002","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F3_23"],"note":"breathing-systems","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During a long anaesthetic using a circle system, the capnograph baseline (inspired CO<sub>2</sub>) gradually rises to 1.5 kPa.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely cause?</p>","options":["Bronchospasm","Exhausted CO<sub>2</sub> absorbent","Hyperventilation","Leak around the tracheal tube cuff","Malignant hyperthermia"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>A rising <strong>inspired</strong> CO<sub>2</sub> means <strong>rebreathing</strong>. In a circle, the commonest cause is <strong>exhausted soda lime</strong> (check the colour indicator); an incompetent unidirectional valve is another cause. Increasing fresh gas flow is a temporary fix.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>E</strong> raises end-tidal CO<sub>2</sub>, not the inspired baseline (unless the absorbent is also exhausted).</li></ul>"},{"id":"px-vent-001","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F1_19"],"note":"ventilators","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient is ventilated in pressure-controlled mode. After abdominal insufflation for laparoscopy, respiratory system compliance falls.</p>\n<p>What happens to tidal volume and peak airway pressure?</p>","options":["Tidal volume falls; peak pressure is unchanged","Tidal volume falls; peak pressure rises","Tidal volume is unchanged; peak pressure rises","Tidal volume rises; peak pressure falls","Both are unchanged"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>A <strong>pressure generator</strong> delivers the set pressure, so the peak pressure stays the same, but with lower compliance, <strong>less volume</strong> is delivered. In volume control, tidal volume would be maintained and pressure would rise (option C).</p>"},{"id":"px-elec-001","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F1_10"],"note":"electricity-safety","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient has a central venous catheter with a saline-filled column in contact with the right atrium.</p>\n<p>What approximate current passing directly to the heart could cause ventricular fibrillation (microshock)?</p>","options":["1 µA","100 µA","10 mA","100 mA","1 A"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>When current enters the heart directly, as little as about <strong>100–150 µA</strong> can cause VF. That is why equipment for direct cardiac connection must be <strong>type CF</strong> (patient leakage ≤ 10 µA). About 100 mA is needed through the intact body (macroshock).</p>"},{"id":"px-elec-002","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F1_11"],"note":"electricity-safety","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which symbol and classification should be carried by a pressure transducer connected to a pulmonary artery catheter?</p>","options":["Class II, type B","Class III, type B","Type BF","Type CF","Any type, provided the device is earthed"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p><strong>Type CF</strong> (cardiac, floating) applied parts have the lowest permitted leakage currents (about 10 µA in normal conditions) and are required for equipment with a direct conductive connection to the heart.</p>"},{"id":"px-elec-003","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F2_19"],"note":"electricity-safety","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>How does the impedance of a capacitor change as the frequency of alternating current increases?</p>","options":["It falls","It rises","It is unchanged","It becomes infinite","It depends only on the voltage"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Capacitive reactance = 1/(2πfC): <strong>impedance falls as frequency rises</strong>, so capacitors pass high-frequency signals and block DC. Inductor impedance does the opposite (2πfL).</p>"},{"id":"px-dia-001","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F1_16"],"note":"diathermy-lasers-fires","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Why does surgical diathermy current not cause ventricular fibrillation, whereas mains current can?</p>","options":["Diathermy uses direct current","Diathermy uses very high-frequency current that does not have time to depolarise excitable tissue","Diathermy current is very small","The patient plate insulates the patient from earth","The voltage used is too low"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Diathermy uses <strong>radiofrequency (around 0.4–1 MHz)</strong> alternating current. At these frequencies excitable membranes cannot respond, so there is no nerve or muscle stimulation or VF, but high current density at the active electrode generates heat.</p>"},{"id":"px-dia-002","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F1_13","1_GA_L_7"],"note":"diathermy-lasers-fires","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>For CO<sub>2</sub> laser surgery to the vocal cords using a laser-resistant tracheal tube, which measure most reduces the risk of an airway fire?</p>","options":["Filling the cuff with air","Using 50% nitrous oxide in oxygen","Using the lowest FiO<sub>2</sub> compatible with safe oxygenation, avoiding nitrous oxide","Using a PVC tracheal tube","Using 100% oxygen to maintain saturation"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Fire needs fuel, an oxidiser and ignition. <strong>Oxygen and N<sub>2</sub>O both support combustion</strong>, so use the <strong>lowest FiO<sub>2</sub></strong> possible (ideally ≤ 0.3 with air), fill the cuff with <strong>saline</strong>, and use a laser-resistant tube. PVC tubes are flammable.</p>"},{"id":"px-defib-001","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F1_15"],"note":"defibrillation-pacing","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A defibrillator's capacitor of 32 µF is charged to 5000 V.</p>\n<p>What energy is stored?</p>","options":["80 J","160 J","400 J","800 J","1600 J"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Stored energy = <strong>½CV<sup>2</sup></strong> = 0.5 × 32 × 10<sup>−6</sup> × (5000)<sup>2</sup> = 0.5 × 32 × 10<sup>−6</sup> × 25 × 10<sup>6</sup> = <strong>400 J</strong>.</p>"},{"id":"px-defib-002","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F1_14"],"note":"defibrillation-pacing","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A pacemaker is programmed in DDD mode.</p>\n<p>What does the third letter indicate?</p>","options":["Both chambers are paced","Both chambers are sensed","Sensing can both inhibit and trigger pacing","The device has rate modulation","The device is an ICD"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>In the NBG code, position I = chamber(s) paced, II = chamber(s) sensed, <strong>III = response to sensing</strong> (O none, I inhibit, T trigger, <strong>D both</strong>). Rate modulation is shown by R in position IV.</p>"},{"id":"px-us-001","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F2_20","1_RA_J_1"],"note":"ultrasound-doppler","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>When performing an ultrasound-guided nerve block at a depth of 1–2 cm, why is a high-frequency (for example 12 MHz) linear probe preferred?</p>","options":["Higher frequencies penetrate deeper","Higher frequencies give better resolution; penetration is adequate at this depth","Higher frequencies increase the Doppler shift","Higher frequencies reduce acoustic shadowing","Linear probes use lower power"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Higher frequencies have shorter wavelengths, giving <strong>better axial resolution</strong>, at the cost of greater <strong>attenuation</strong> (less penetration). For superficial structures, penetration is not limiting.</p>"},{"id":"px-us-002","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F2_22"],"note":"ultrasound-doppler","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>When measuring blood velocity by Doppler, the angle between the ultrasound beam and the direction of flow increases from 0° to 60°.</p>\n<p>What happens to the measured Doppler shift for the same true velocity?</p>","options":["It doubles","It halves","It is unchanged","It falls to zero","It reverses direction"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The Doppler shift ∝ <strong>cos θ</strong>. cos 0° = 1; cos 60° = 0.5, so the shift <strong>halves</strong>. If the angle is not corrected for, velocity is underestimated; at 90° there is no shift.</p>"},{"id":"px-o2-001","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_C_2"],"note":"oxygen-delivery-devices","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which device delivers a fixed and predictable FiO<sub>2</sub>, independent of the patient's breathing pattern, provided its flow exceeds peak inspiratory flow?</p>","options":["Nasal cannulae at 4 L/min","Non-rebreathe mask with reservoir at 15 L/min","Simple face mask at 6 L/min","Venturi mask","Tracheostomy mask at 5 L/min"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p><strong>Venturi masks</strong> entrain a fixed ratio of air to oxygen, giving a <strong>fixed-performance</strong> FiO<sub>2</sub> provided total flow exceeds the patient's peak inspiratory flow. The others are variable-performance devices.</p>"},{"id":"px-inf-001","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F1_22"],"note":"infusion-decontamination","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A noradrenaline infusion is made up as 4 mg in 50 mL. The prescribed dose is 0.05 micrograms/kg/min for an 80 kg patient.</p>\n<p>What infusion rate is needed?</p>","options":["0.3 mL/h","1.5 mL/h","3 mL/h","6 mL/h","30 mL/h"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Concentration = 4000 µg / 50 mL = <strong>80 µg/mL</strong>. Dose = 0.05 × 80 = 4 µg/min = <strong>240 µg/h</strong>. Rate = 240 / 80 = <strong>3 mL/h</strong>.</p>"},{"id":"px-inf-002","subject":"physics","codes":["1_GA_F1_24"],"note":"infusion-decontamination","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which process reliably destroys bacterial spores?</p>","options":["Cleaning with detergent","Low-level disinfection with 70% alcohol","Pasteurisation at 70°C","Steam autoclaving at 134°C for 3 minutes","Washing with chlorhexidine"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p><strong>Sterilisation</strong> (e.g. steam autoclave at 134°C for 3 minutes) kills all micro-organisms <strong>including spores</strong>. Disinfection and cleaning do not reliably destroy spores; prions resist even standard sterilisation.</p>"},{"id":"ph-lv-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_58","1_GA_F3_4"],"note":"resp-lung-volumes","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 60-year-old man with severe emphysema has lung volumes measured before lung volume reduction surgery. Total lung capacity by helium dilution is considerably lower than total lung capacity by body plethysmography.</p>\n<p>Which is the best explanation for the difference?</p>","options":["Helium is more soluble in blood than nitrogen","Helium dilution does not measure gas in poorly communicating airspaces such as bullae","Plethysmography overestimates volumes because of airway resistance","Residual volume cannot be measured by plethysmography","The patient hyperventilated during the helium test"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Helium dilution measures only gas that <strong>communicates</strong> with the airways during the test. Gas trapped behind closed airways or in bullae is missed. Body plethysmography (Boyle's law) measures <strong>all</strong> compressible intrathoracic gas, so it is larger in emphysema.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> Helium is chosen precisely because it is very insoluble.</li><li><strong>C</strong> Plethysmography measures thoracic gas volume; it does not systematically overestimate because of resistance.</li><li><strong>D</strong> Plethysmography measures FRC (thoracic gas volume), from which RV is derived.</li><li><strong>E</strong> Hyperventilation would not explain a consistent difference in trapped gas.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-lv-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_58","1_GA_B_18"],"note":"resp-lung-volumes","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Two adults are pre-oxygenated to an end-tidal oxygen fraction of 0.9 and then become apnoeic with a patent airway. Patient X is a 70 kg man; patient Y is a woman at 38 weeks' gestation.</p>\n<p>Which combination best explains why patient Y desaturates first?</p>","options":["Higher closing capacity and lower cardiac output","Lower functional residual capacity and higher oxygen consumption","Lower haemoglobin concentration and a left-shifted dissociation curve","Lower minute ventilation and higher dead space","Reduced diffusion capacity and increased shunt fraction"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The time to desaturation during apnoea depends mainly on the <strong>oxygen store (FRC × alveolar FO<sub>2</sub>)</strong> divided by <strong>oxygen consumption</strong>. At term, FRC falls (about 20%, more when supine) and oxygen consumption rises (roughly 20–30%), so the store is used up much faster.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> Cardiac output is increased in pregnancy, not reduced.</li><li><strong>C</strong> The maternal dissociation curve shifts slightly <strong>right</strong>; physiological anaemia contributes little to apnoea time.</li><li><strong>D</strong> Minute ventilation is increased in pregnancy, and ventilation is irrelevant during apnoea.</li><li><strong>E</strong> Diffusion capacity is essentially unchanged at term.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-mech-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_58","1_GA_E_2"],"note":"resp-mechanics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A ventilated patient receives a tidal volume of 500 mL with PEEP of 5 cmH<sub>2</sub>O. Peak airway pressure is 40 cmH<sub>2</sub>O and plateau pressure is 15 cmH<sub>2</sub>O during an inspiratory pause.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely cause of the high peak pressure?</p>","options":["Bronchospasm","Endobronchial intubation","Pneumoperitoneum","Pulmonary oedema","Tension pneumothorax"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>A large gap between <strong>peak</strong> and <strong>plateau</strong> pressure means a high <strong>resistance</strong> to flow. The plateau (no flow) reflects compliance, here normal: static compliance = 500 / (15 − 5) = 50 mL/cmH<sub>2</sub>O, reasonable under anaesthesia. Bronchospasm, secretions or a kinked tube raise the peak but not the plateau.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>B, C, D, E</strong> all reduce <strong>compliance</strong>, so they raise the plateau pressure as well as the peak.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-mech-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_F2_18"],"note":"resp-mechanics","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>In the lungs of a preterm neonate born at 28 weeks' gestation, which consequence of surfactant deficiency is best explained by Laplace's law?</p>","options":["Increased airway resistance in the main bronchi","Reduced hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","The tendency of small alveoli to collapse into larger ones","Increased anatomical dead space","Reduced carbon dioxide diffusion across the alveolar membrane"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>For a sphere, <strong>P = 2T/r</strong>. With a uniform surface tension, the pressure inside a small alveolus is higher than in a large one, so small alveoli would empty into large ones. Surfactant lowers surface tension more as the alveolus shrinks, stabilising alveoli of different sizes.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> Bronchial resistance is not caused by surface tension.</li><li><strong>B</strong> HPV is a vascular response to alveolar hypoxia.</li><li><strong>D</strong> Anatomical dead space relates to the conducting airways.</li><li><strong>E</strong> CO<sub>2</sub> diffuses very readily; this is not a Laplace effect.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-vq-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_60","1_GA_G2_56"],"note":"resp-vq","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with right lower lobe pneumonia has an SpO<sub>2</sub> of 86% breathing air. Increasing the inspired oxygen to 60% only raises the SpO<sub>2</sub> to 89%.</p>\n<p>Which mechanism best explains the poor response to oxygen?</p>","options":["Alveolar hypoventilation","Diffusion impairment","Increased alveolar dead space","Low inspired oxygen partial pressure","Shunt"],"answer":4,"explain":"<p>Blood flowing through consolidated, unventilated lung (V/Q = 0) never meets the enriched gas, so raising FiO<sub>2</sub> barely helps once shunt is large. Blood from normal units is already nearly fully saturated and carries little extra oxygen.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong>, <strong>B</strong> and <strong>D</strong> are all readily corrected by increasing FiO<sub>2</sub>.</li><li><strong>C</strong> Dead space (ventilation without perfusion) raises PaCO<sub>2</sub> and the ETCO<sub>2</sub> gap rather than causing refractory hypoxaemia.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-vq-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_56"],"note":"resp-vq","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient is breathing 40% oxygen at sea level (barometric pressure 101 kPa, saturated water vapour pressure 6.3 kPa). The PaCO<sub>2</sub> is 5.0 kPa and the respiratory quotient is 0.8.</p>\n<p>What is the approximate alveolar PO<sub>2</sub>?</p>","options":["13 kPa","20 kPa","26 kPa","32 kPa","38 kPa"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p><strong>P<sub>A</sub>O<sub>2</sub> = F<sub>I</sub>O<sub>2</sub> (P<sub>B</sub> − P<sub>H2O</sub>) − P<sub>a</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>/RQ</strong> = 0.4 × (101 − 6.3) − 5.0/0.8 = 37.9 − 6.25 ≈ <strong>31.6 kPa</strong>.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>E</strong> forgets to subtract the CO<sub>2</sub> term.</li><li><strong>A</strong> is the value for breathing air.</li><li><strong>B</strong> and <strong>C</strong> are arithmetic errors.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-o2-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_56","1_GA_G2_57"],"note":"resp-o2-transport","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 65-year-old man is anaemic (haemoglobin 70 g/L) after gastrointestinal bleeding. His SpO<sub>2</sub> is 98% and PaO<sub>2</sub> is 12 kPa. His cardiac output is unchanged.</p>\n<p>Compared with his previous haemoglobin of 140 g/L, which change is most accurate?</p>","options":["Arterial oxygen content is approximately halved","Arterial oxygen saturation will fall to about 75%","Dissolved oxygen falls by half","Oxygen delivery is unchanged because PaO<sub>2</sub> is normal","The carotid bodies will be strongly stimulated"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Oxygen content is dominated by haemoglobin-bound oxygen: <strong>1.34 × Hb × SaO<sub>2</sub></strong>. Halving Hb roughly halves CaO<sub>2</sub> (from about 19 to about 9.5 mL/dL); with unchanged cardiac output, delivery halves too.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>B</strong> Saturation depends on PaO<sub>2</sub>, which is normal.</li><li><strong>C</strong> Dissolved oxygen depends on PaO<sub>2</sub>, not haemoglobin.</li><li><strong>D</strong> Delivery depends on content, not PaO<sub>2</sub> alone.</li><li><strong>E</strong> The carotid bodies sense PaO<sub>2</sub>, not content, so anaemia does not stimulate them.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-o2-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_57","1_GA_G2_23"],"note":"resp-o2-transport","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient receives a massive transfusion of red cells stored for 30 days.</p>\n<p>Which change to the oxyhaemoglobin dissociation curve is most likely in the transfused cells immediately after transfusion?</p>","options":["Left shift because of depleted 2,3-diphosphoglycerate","Left shift because of hyperkalaemia","No change because the P50 of stored blood is normal","Right shift because of the acidity of stored blood","Right shift because of raised 2,3-diphosphoglycerate"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>2,3-DPG falls progressively in stored red cells, increasing oxygen affinity (<strong>left shift</strong>, lower P50), so oxygen unloading in the tissues is initially impaired. Levels recover over hours to days after transfusion.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>B</strong> Potassium does not directly shift the curve.</li><li><strong>C</strong> P50 of stored blood is reduced.</li><li><strong>D</strong> Stored blood is acidic, but the dominant effect on the transfused cells' affinity is 2,3-DPG depletion.</li><li><strong>E</strong> 2,3-DPG is depleted, not raised.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-co2-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_56","1_GA_G2_57"],"note":"resp-co2-transport","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Venous blood carries considerably more carbon dioxide than arterial blood, yet venous pH is only slightly lower than arterial pH.</p>\n<p>Which mechanism contributes most to limiting the fall in pH?</p>","options":["Carbonic anhydrase activity in plasma","Dissolution of carbon dioxide in plasma water","Increased buffering capacity of deoxygenated haemoglobin","The Bohr effect","Renal excretion of hydrogen ions"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>This is the <strong>Haldane effect</strong>. Deoxyhaemoglobin is a weaker acid and a better buffer for H<sup>+</sup>, and forms carbamino compounds more readily, so CO<sub>2</sub> can be loaded in the tissues with little change in pH.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> Carbonic anhydrase is mainly in red cells; it speeds CO<sub>2</sub> hydration but generates H<sup>+</sup> rather than buffering it.</li><li><strong>B</strong> Dissolved CO<sub>2</sub> accounts for only a small fraction of transport.</li><li><strong>D</strong> The Bohr effect describes the effect of CO<sub>2</sub>/H<sup>+</sup> on oxygen binding.</li><li><strong>E</strong> Renal compensation is far too slow to act across a single capillary transit.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-ctrl-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_61"],"note":"resp-control","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with carbon monoxide poisoning has a carboxyhaemoglobin level of 30%. PaO<sub>2</sub> is 13 kPa breathing air.</p>\n<p>Why is there no hyperventilation in response to the reduced oxygen content?</p>","options":["Carbon monoxide directly depresses the medullary respiratory centre","Central chemoreceptors respond only to cerebrospinal fluid bicarbonate","Peripheral chemoreceptors respond to arterial PO<sub>2</sub> rather than oxygen content","The carotid bodies are denervated by hypoxia","The oxyhaemoglobin curve is shifted to the right"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The carotid bodies have a very high blood flow relative to their metabolism and sense <strong>dissolved oxygen (PaO<sub>2</sub>)</strong>, which is normal here. Reduced content from CO poisoning or anaemia does not stimulate them.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> Not the main mechanism.</li><li><strong>B</strong> Central chemoreceptors respond to CSF H<sup>+</sup> (from CO<sub>2</sub>).</li><li><strong>D</strong> Hypoxia stimulates, not denervates, the carotid bodies.</li><li><strong>E</strong> CO shifts the curve to the <strong>left</strong>.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-ctrl-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_61","1_GA_G3_50"],"note":"resp-control","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which change in the carbon dioxide ventilatory response curve is characteristic of a therapeutic dose of intravenous morphine?</p>","options":["Parallel shift to the left","Rightward shift with reduced slope","Rightward shift with increased slope","Steeper slope without shift","No change until PaCO<sub>2</sub> exceeds 10 kPa"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Opioids depress the brainstem response to CO<sub>2</sub>: the curve moves <strong>right</strong> (a higher PaCO<sub>2</sub> is needed for the same ventilation) and becomes <strong>flatter</strong>. Hypoxia, by contrast, increases the slope.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> A left shift occurs with stimulants and in metabolic acidosis.</li><li><strong>C</strong> and <strong>D</strong> are not typical of opioids.</li><li><strong>E</strong> is not a recognised pattern.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-pc-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_63"],"note":"resp-pulmonary-circulation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which of the following substances is largely inactivated or removed in a single passage through the pulmonary circulation?</p>","options":["Adrenaline","Angiotensin II","Bradykinin","Histamine","Isoprenaline"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The pulmonary endothelium inactivates about 80% of <strong>bradykinin</strong> (by angiotensin-converting enzyme) and removes much of noradrenaline and serotonin.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong>, <strong>B</strong>, <strong>D</strong> and <strong>E</strong> pass through the lung largely unchanged. (ACE converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II; angiotensin II itself is not inactivated.)</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-ippv-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_59"],"note":"resp-ippv","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A hypovolaemic patient becomes hypotensive immediately after tracheal intubation and the start of positive pressure ventilation with PEEP of 10 cmH<sub>2</sub>O.</p>\n<p>Which mechanism is most important?</p>","options":["Increased left ventricular afterload","Increased pulmonary vascular resistance from hypoxic vasoconstriction","Reduced systemic venous return due to raised intrathoracic pressure","Reflex bradycardia from laryngoscopy","Release of atrial natriuretic peptide"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Positive intrathoracic pressure raises right atrial pressure and reduces the gradient for <strong>venous return</strong>; a hypovolaemic patient with a low mean systemic filling pressure is especially sensitive. Induction agents also reduce venous tone.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> IPPV <strong>reduces</strong> LV afterload.</li><li><strong>B</strong> Oxygenation after intubation should reduce HPV, not increase it.</li><li><strong>D</strong> Laryngoscopy usually causes a pressor response, although bradycardia can occur in children.</li><li><strong>E</strong> ANP release is not an acute cause of hypotension.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-alt-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_62","1_GA_F1_8"],"note":"resp-altitude","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A sevoflurane variable-bypass vaporiser calibrated at sea level is used at an altitude where barometric pressure is 70 kPa. The dial is set to 2%.</p>\n<p>Which statement is most accurate?</p>","options":["The delivered partial pressure of sevoflurane is about 30% lower than at sea level","The delivered partial pressure is about the same as at sea level, although the percentage is higher","The delivered percentage is lower, so the dial must be turned up","The vaporiser will not function below 80 kPa","The saturated vapour pressure of sevoflurane falls with altitude"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Saturated vapour pressure depends only on temperature, so the vaporising chamber still produces the same <strong>partial pressure</strong> of agent. Diluted by the same splitting ratio, the output partial pressure is roughly unchanged, but because total pressure is lower, the <strong>percentage</strong> is higher. Anaesthetic effect depends on partial pressure, so the usual dial setting gives a similar effect.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> and <strong>C</strong> confuse percentage with partial pressure.</li><li><strong>D</strong> Vaporisers work at altitude.</li><li><strong>E</strong> SVP is independent of ambient pressure.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-cc-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_38"],"note":"cvs-cardiac-cycle","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>On a left ventricular pressure–volume loop, which feature best represents myocardial contractility relatively independently of loading conditions?</p>","options":["The area of the loop","The ejection fraction","The slope of the end-diastolic pressure–volume relationship","The slope of the end-systolic pressure–volume relationship","The width of the loop"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>The <strong>end-systolic pressure–volume relationship (ESPVR)</strong>, obtained by varying load, has a slope (end-systolic elastance) that reflects contractility and is relatively load-independent.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> Area is stroke work.</li><li><strong>B</strong> Ejection fraction is useful clinically but depends on preload and afterload.</li><li><strong>C</strong> EDPVR reflects diastolic stiffness.</li><li><strong>E</strong> Width is stroke volume.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-cc-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_38","1_GA_F3_11"],"note":"cvs-cardiac-cycle","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient has a central venous pressure trace showing large regular waves occurring as the right atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve.</p>\n<p>Which rhythm is most likely?</p>","options":["Atrial fibrillation","Complete heart block","Sinus tachycardia","Tricuspid regurgitation in sinus rhythm","Wenckebach (Mobitz I) block"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Cannon a waves</strong> occur when the atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve. In <strong>complete heart block</strong> (AV dissociation) this happens intermittently and irregularly as atrial and ventricular contractions coincide; regular cannon waves occur in junctional rhythms and some ventricular tachycardias.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> AF abolishes a waves.</li><li><strong>C</strong> Sinus tachycardia may merge a and v waves but does not cause cannon waves.</li><li><strong>D</strong> Tricuspid regurgitation produces large <strong>v</strong> waves.</li><li><strong>E</strong> Wenckebach block does not typically cause cannon waves.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-co-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_41","1_GA_G2_42"],"note":"cvs-cardiac-output","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During a fluid challenge, 250 mL of crystalloid is given over 5 minutes to a ventilated patient. Stroke volume measured by oesophageal Doppler rises from 60 mL to 72 mL.</p>\n<p>What does this response most likely indicate?</p>","options":["The left ventricle is operating on the flat part of its Starling curve","The patient is fluid responsive and on the steep part of the Starling curve","Myocardial contractility has increased","Systemic vascular resistance has fallen","The patient has become fluid overloaded"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>A <strong>20% rise in stroke volume</strong> (more than the commonly used 10% threshold) after a modest bolus shows that preload increased stroke volume: the ventricle is on the <strong>steep</strong> part of the Frank–Starling curve.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> On the flat part, stroke volume would not rise.</li><li><strong>C</strong> A fluid bolus changes preload, not contractility.</li><li><strong>D</strong> Not implied by a stroke volume response.</li><li><strong>E</strong> Overload would be suggested by no stroke volume response with rising filling pressures.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-co-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_40"],"note":"cvs-cardiac-output","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>According to Laplace's law, which change would increase left ventricular wall stress during ejection, other factors being equal?</p>","options":["A reduction in systolic blood pressure","Concentric left ventricular hypertrophy","Dilatation of the left ventricle","Reduced end-diastolic volume","An increase in wall thickness"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Wall stress ∝ <strong>P × r / 2h</strong>. A larger radius (dilated ventricle) increases wall stress, and therefore afterload and oxygen demand, at the same pressure.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> and <strong>D</strong> reduce wall stress.</li><li><strong>B</strong> and <strong>E</strong> Wall thickening reduces wall stress, which is why hypertrophy is an adaptive response to pressure overload.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-ap-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_39"],"note":"cvs-cardiac-ap","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which ionic current is chiefly responsible for the upstroke (phase 0) of the sinoatrial node action potential?</p>","options":["Fast sodium influx","L-type calcium influx","Potassium efflux through the delayed rectifier","The funny current carried by HCN channels","T-type calcium influx"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Nodal cells have a less negative maximum diastolic potential (about −60 mV), at which fast Na<sup>+</sup> channels are largely inactivated, so the upstroke is due to <strong>L-type calcium</strong> channels, and it is slower than in ventricular muscle.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> drives phase 0 in atrial and ventricular myocytes and Purkinje fibres.</li><li><strong>C</strong> repolarises.</li><li><strong>D</strong> and <strong>E</strong> contribute to slow phase 4 (diastolic) depolarisation.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-ap-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_37","1_GA_G2_9"],"note":"cvs-cardiac-ap","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with acute kidney injury has a plasma potassium of 7.4 mmol/L and a broad QRS complex.</p>\n<p>What is the main mechanism by which hyperkalaemia slows conduction in ventricular muscle?</p>","options":["Hyperpolarisation of the resting membrane potential","Increased L-type calcium channel opening","Partial depolarisation of the resting membrane, inactivating fast sodium channels","Prolongation of the plateau phase","Reduced activity of the Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Raised extracellular K<sup>+</sup> reduces the K<sup>+</sup> gradient, making the resting potential <strong>less negative</strong>. At a less negative potential, a proportion of fast Na<sup>+</sup> channels are inactivated, so the phase 0 upstroke is slower and conduction slows (wide QRS). Intravenous calcium stabilises the membrane by raising the threshold potential.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> Hyperkalaemia depolarises, not hyperpolarises.</li><li><strong>B</strong> Not the mechanism.</li><li><strong>D</strong> Hyperkalaemia tends to shorten repolarisation (tall, peaked T waves).</li><li><strong>E</strong> Not the main mechanism of conduction slowing.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-bp-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_44"],"note":"cvs-bp-control","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 70-year-old man with long-standing diabetes performs a Valsalva manoeuvre during preoperative assessment. His blood pressure falls during the strain and there is no overshoot or bradycardia after release.</p>\n<p>What does this suggest?</p>","options":["Aortic stenosis","Autonomic neuropathy","Hypovolaemia with an intact reflex","Left ventricular failure with a square-wave response","Normal ageing"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Loss of the <strong>phase IV overshoot and reflex bradycardia</strong> (and a continued fall in phase II without recovery) indicates failure of the baroreflex arc: <strong>autonomic neuropathy</strong>. Such patients are prone to marked hypotension at induction and with positioning.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>D</strong> In heart failure the <strong>square-wave</strong> pattern shows blood pressure that <strong>rises</strong> and stays up during strain.</li><li><strong>A</strong>, <strong>C</strong>, <strong>E</strong> do not produce this pattern.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-bp-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_44","1_GA_G1_24"],"note":"cvs-bp-control","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which nerve carries afferent impulses from the carotid sinus baroreceptors?</p>","options":["Facial nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Phrenic nerve","Vagus nerve"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The <strong>carotid sinus nerve</strong>, a branch of the <strong>glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)</strong>, carries baroreceptor (and carotid body chemoreceptor) afferents to the nucleus tractus solitarius. The aortic arch receptors travel in the <strong>vagus (X)</strong>.</p>"},{"id":"ph-mc-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_13"],"note":"cvs-microcirculation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>According to the revised Starling principle, which structure largely determines the effective oncotic pressure gradient opposing filtration across a continuous capillary?</p>","options":["The basement membrane","The endothelial glycocalyx","The interstitial matrix far from the capillary","The lymphatic endothelium","The pericyte layer"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The <strong>glycocalyx</strong> acts as the semi-permeable layer. The oncotic pressure that matters is between plasma and the small, protein-poor space just beneath the glycocalyx, not the bulk interstitium. As a result, most capillaries filter along their whole length and the fluid returns via lymph.</p>"},{"id":"ph-sc-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_47"],"note":"cvs-special-circulations","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with severe coronary artery disease develops ST depression when his heart rate rises from 70 to 120 beats per minute under anaesthesia, with an unchanged blood pressure.</p>\n<p>Which is the main reason the subendocardium becomes ischaemic?</p>","options":["Coronary blood flow is autoregulated between 50 and 150 mmHg","Diastolic time falls proportionally more than systolic time, reducing subendocardial perfusion time","Myocardial oxygen extraction is only 25% at rest","Tachycardia reduces left ventricular end-diastolic pressure","The right coronary artery supplies the subendocardium"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Left ventricular subendocardial flow occurs mainly in <strong>diastole</strong>, and diastole shortens much more than systole as heart rate rises. Tachycardia therefore reduces supply while increasing demand. Because extraction is already about 70% at rest, the only way to meet demand is more flow, which diseased vessels cannot provide.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> Autoregulation does not protect against loss of diastolic time.</li><li><strong>C</strong> Extraction is about 70%.</li><li><strong>D</strong> A lower LVEDP would, if anything, improve perfusion pressure.</li><li><strong>E</strong> Incorrect anatomy.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-sc-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_47","1_GA_G2_66"],"note":"cvs-special-circulations","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>In a patient with normal autoregulation, which change is expected to produce the greatest increase in cerebral blood flow?</p>","options":["An increase in mean arterial pressure from 80 to 110 mmHg","An increase in PaCO<sub>2</sub> from 5 to 8 kPa","An increase in PaO<sub>2</sub> from 12 to 30 kPa","Induction of anaesthesia with propofol","Mild hypothermia"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Within about 3–10 kPa, cerebral blood flow rises roughly linearly with PaCO<sub>2</sub> (about 15 mL/100 g/min per kPa): a rise from 5 to 8 kPa would increase flow by around 80–90%.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> is within the autoregulatory range, so flow changes little.</li><li><strong>C</strong> Hyperoxia causes mild vasoconstriction.</li><li><strong>D</strong> and <strong>E</strong> reduce metabolic rate and flow.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-fetal-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_90","1_GA_G2_11"],"note":"cvs-fetal-circulation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A term neonate becomes hypoxic, hypercapnic and acidotic after birth.</p>\n<p>Which change is most likely to occur as a result?</p>","options":["Accelerated closure of the ductus arteriosus","Increased left-to-right shunting across the foramen ovale","Increased pulmonary vascular resistance with right-to-left shunting","Reduced systemic vascular resistance causing closure of the ductus venosus","Rightward shift of the fetal haemoglobin dissociation curve to normal adult values"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Hypoxia, hypercapnia and acidosis raise <strong>pulmonary vascular resistance</strong>. Right-sided pressures rise and blood can shunt <strong>right-to-left</strong> through a still-patent foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus: a return towards the fetal (transitional) circulation, with worsening hypoxaemia.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> Hypoxia tends to keep the ductus open.</li><li><strong>B</strong> Shunting reverses to right-to-left.</li><li><strong>D</strong> and <strong>E</strong> are not the typical consequences.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-map-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_28","1_GA_G2_64"],"note":"neuro-membrane-ap","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>An isolated myelinated nerve is bathed in a solution containing sodium, potassium and chloride ions.</p>\n<p>Which change would make the resting membrane potential least negative?</p>","options":["Doubling extracellular chloride concentration","Doubling extracellular potassium concentration","Doubling extracellular sodium concentration","Halving extracellular potassium concentration","Halving extracellular sodium concentration"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>At rest the membrane is far more permeable to K<sup>+</sup> than to other ions, so the resting potential follows the <strong>potassium equilibrium potential</strong> (Nernst). Raising extracellular K<sup>+</sup> reduces the gradient and <strong>depolarises</strong> the membrane.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong>, <strong>C</strong> and <strong>E</strong> Chloride and sodium permeabilities at rest are low, so changes have little effect.</li><li><strong>D</strong> Lowering extracellular K<sup>+</sup> hyperpolarises the membrane.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-map-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_64","1_GA_G3_46"],"note":"neuro-membrane-ap","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>After a spinal anaesthetic, the block to cold sensation extends to T4, the block to light touch to T6, and the motor block to T10.</p>\n<p>Which is the best explanation for this pattern?</p>","options":["Cold fibres are larger than motor fibres","Motor fibres run in the anterior root, which is further from the local anaesthetic","Smaller-diameter fibres are blocked at lower local anaesthetic concentrations","Touch fibres are unmyelinated","The sympathetic chain lies outside the subarachnoid space"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The concentration of local anaesthetic falls with distance from the site of injection. Thin fibres (Aδ and C for temperature and pain; B for sympathetic) are blocked by lower concentrations than thick fibres (Aβ touch, then Aα motor), so their blocks extend higher.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> Cold is carried by thin Aδ fibres, motor by large Aα fibres.</li><li><strong>B</strong> Both roots are bathed in CSF; this is not the explanation.</li><li><strong>D</strong> Touch is carried by myelinated Aβ fibres.</li><li><strong>E</strong> Sympathetic block above the sensory level arises from preganglionic B fibres in the roots, which are bathed in CSF.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-nmj-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_29","1_GA_G3_51"],"note":"neuro-nmj-muscle","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 30-year-old man with 40% total body surface area burns needs surgery three weeks after his injury.</p>\n<p>Which change at the neuromuscular junction explains the risk of hyperkalaemia with suxamethonium?</p>","options":["Downregulation of acetylcholinesterase","Increased release of acetylcholine","Proliferation of extrajunctional acetylcholine receptors","Reduced plasma cholinesterase activity","Sensitisation of the ryanodine receptor"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>After burns, denervation and prolonged immobility, <strong>immature (γ-subunit) and α7 receptors spread across the muscle membrane</strong>. Suxamethonium opens them all, causing a large K<sup>+</sup> efflux, which can be fatal. The risk begins from about 24–48 hours after injury and persists for months.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong>, <strong>B</strong> and <strong>D</strong> do not explain the hyperkalaemia.</li><li><strong>E</strong> relates to malignant hyperthermia.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-nmj-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_30","1_GA_G2_31"],"note":"neuro-nmj-muscle","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 58-year-old smoker with small-cell lung cancer has proximal weakness that improves briefly with repeated effort.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely effect of this condition on his response to neuromuscular blocking drugs?</p>","options":["Normal sensitivity to both depolarising and non-depolarising drugs","Resistance to non-depolarising drugs only","Resistance to suxamethonium and sensitivity to non-depolarising drugs","Sensitivity to both depolarising and non-depolarising drugs","Sensitivity to suxamethonium only"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>This is <strong>Lambert–Eaton myasthenic syndrome</strong>: antibodies to presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels reduce ACh release. Patients are <strong>sensitive to both</strong> depolarising and non-depolarising blockers.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>C</strong> describes <strong>myasthenia gravis</strong> (fewer postsynaptic receptors).</li><li><strong>A</strong>, <strong>B</strong> and <strong>E</strong> are incorrect.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-icp-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_66"],"note":"neuro-icp-cbf","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with a traumatic brain injury has a mean arterial pressure of 85 mmHg, an intracranial pressure of 25 mmHg and a central venous pressure of 10 mmHg.</p>\n<p>What is the cerebral perfusion pressure?</p>","options":["50 mmHg","60 mmHg","75 mmHg","85 mmHg","110 mmHg"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>CPP = MAP − ICP</strong> (use whichever is higher of ICP and CVP): 85 − 25 = <strong>60 mmHg</strong>.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>C</strong> subtracts CVP, which is lower than ICP here.</li><li><strong>A</strong> subtracts both.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-icp-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_16","1_GA_G1_14"],"note":"neuro-icp-cbf","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Compared with plasma, which is the best description of normal lumbar cerebrospinal fluid?</p>","options":["Higher chloride and lower glucose","Higher glucose and higher protein","Higher potassium and lower sodium","Higher protein and higher bicarbonate","Identical composition apart from absence of cells"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>CSF is actively secreted, not an ultrafiltrate. It has a <strong>higher chloride</strong>, <strong>lower glucose (about 60% of plasma)</strong>, lower potassium, very little protein and a similar sodium concentration.</p>"},{"id":"ph-ans-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_67","1_GA_G1_20"],"note":"neuro-ans","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which of the following sympathetic fibres releases acetylcholine at its target organ?</p>","options":["Fibres to arteriolar smooth muscle in skin","Fibres to cardiac pacemaker cells","Fibres to eccrine sweat glands","Fibres to the dilator pupillae","Fibres to the kidney juxtaglomerular cells"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Postganglionic sympathetic fibres to <strong>eccrine sweat glands</strong> are cholinergic (muscarinic receptors). Fibres to the adrenal medulla are preganglionic and also cholinergic.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong>, <strong>B</strong>, <strong>D</strong> and <strong>E</strong> release noradrenaline.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-ans-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G1_23"],"note":"neuro-ans","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A stellate ganglion block is performed for upper limb sympathetically maintained pain.</p>\n<p>Which sign best confirms a successful sympathetic block?</p>","options":["Bradycardia","Ipsilateral mydriasis","Ipsilateral ptosis and miosis","Loss of the corneal reflex","Weakness of the diaphragm"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Interrupting cervical sympathetic outflow produces <strong>Horner's syndrome</strong>: ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis (and enophthalmos, facial flushing, nasal congestion), along with a warmer ipsilateral hand.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>B</strong> Sympathetic block causes miosis, not mydriasis.</li><li><strong>E</strong> Phrenic nerve block is a complication, not a sign of success.</li><li><strong>A</strong> and <strong>D</strong> are not expected.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-pain-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_70","1_GA_G1_11"],"note":"neuro-pain-physiology","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which receptor on dorsal horn neurones is most closely associated with \"wind-up\" and central sensitisation after repeated C-fibre stimulation?</p>","options":["AMPA glutamate receptor","GABA<sub>A</sub> receptor","μ-opioid receptor","NMDA glutamate receptor","5-HT<sub>3</sub> receptor"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Sustained C-fibre input depolarises dorsal horn neurones enough to remove the <strong>magnesium block of NMDA receptors</strong>, amplifying responses (wind-up). This is the rationale for NMDA antagonists such as ketamine in some pain states.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> AMPA receptors mediate fast baseline transmission.</li><li><strong>B</strong> and <strong>C</strong> are inhibitory.</li><li><strong>E</strong> is not central to wind-up.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-pain-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G1_12","1_PA_F_4"],"note":"neuro-pain-physiology","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which spinal segments carry most of the visceral pain of the first stage of labour?</p>","options":["C5–T1","T4–T6","T10–L1","L4–S1","S2–S4"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Pain from uterine contractions and cervical dilatation travels with sympathetic afferents to <strong>T10–L1</strong>. The second stage adds somatic pain from the vagina and perineum via the pudendal nerve (<strong>S2–S4</strong>, option E).</p>"},{"id":"ph-gfr-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_49","1_GA_G2_51"],"note":"renal-gfr","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A substance X has a plasma concentration of 2 mg/mL, a urine concentration of 250 mg/mL and a urine flow of 1 mL/min.</p>\n<p>What is its renal clearance?</p>","options":["0.008 mL/min","2 mL/min","125 mL/min","250 mL/min","500 mL/min"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p><strong>Clearance = (U × V) / P</strong> = (250 × 1) / 2 = <strong>125 mL/min</strong>. If X is freely filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted (like inulin), this equals the GFR.</p>"},{"id":"ph-gfr-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_48"],"note":"renal-gfr","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient taking an ACE inhibitor becomes hypovolaemic after bowel preparation and develops acute kidney injury.</p>\n<p>Which mechanism best explains the fall in GFR?</p>","options":["Afferent arteriolar dilatation","Increased tubuloglomerular feedback","Loss of angiotensin II-mediated efferent arteriolar constriction","Reduced plasma oncotic pressure","Reduced renin secretion"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>When renal perfusion falls, <strong>angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole</strong>, maintaining glomerular capillary pressure and GFR. ACE inhibitors remove this support, so GFR falls sharply in hypovolaemia.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> Afferent dilatation would increase GFR.</li><li><strong>E</strong> Renin rises with ACE inhibition.</li><li><strong>B</strong> and <strong>D</strong> are not the main mechanism.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-tub-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_50","1_GA_G3_73"],"note":"renal-tubule","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which part of the nephron is impermeable to water and reabsorbs about a quarter of filtered sodium by a transporter inhibited by furosemide?</p>","options":["Collecting duct","Distal convoluted tubule","Proximal convoluted tubule","Thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle","Thin descending limb of the loop of Henle"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>The <strong>thick ascending limb</strong> reabsorbs NaCl via the <strong>Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>/2Cl<sup>−</sup> co-transporter</strong> (the target of loop diuretics) while being impermeable to water. This dilutes the tubular fluid and builds the medullary gradient.</p>"},{"id":"ph-tub-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_52","1_GA_G2_84"],"note":"renal-tubule","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Twelve hours after trans-sphenoidal pituitary surgery, a patient passes 400 mL/h of urine with an osmolality of 90 mOsm/kg. Plasma sodium is 149 mmol/L.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>","options":["Cerebral salt wasting","Cranial diabetes insipidus","Osmotic diuresis from mannitol","Physiological diuresis of excess intraoperative fluid","Syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Large volumes of <strong>dilute urine</strong> with a <strong>rising plasma sodium</strong> after pituitary surgery indicate <strong>cranial diabetes insipidus</strong> (lack of ADH). Treatment is desmopressin and careful fluid balance.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> Salt wasting causes hyponatraemia with high urinary sodium.</li><li><strong>C</strong> Osmotic diuresis produces urine that is not maximally dilute (typically near-isotonic).</li><li><strong>D</strong> A physiological diuresis would not raise plasma sodium.</li><li><strong>E</strong> SIADH causes hyponatraemia with concentrated urine.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-ab-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_8","1_GA_F3_14"],"note":"acid-base","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>An arterial blood gas shows pH 7.24, PaCO<sub>2</sub> 3.1 kPa, bicarbonate 10 mmol/L, sodium 138 mmol/L, chloride 102 mmol/L and albumin 40 g/L.</p>\n<p>Which is the most likely cause?</p>","options":["Diarrhoea","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Excess 0.9% sodium chloride","Renal tubular acidosis","Ureterosigmoidostomy"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>There is a metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation. The anion gap (without K<sup>+</sup>) = 138 − (102 + 10) = <strong>26 mmol/L</strong>, which is raised. Of the options, only <strong>ketoacidosis</strong> causes a raised anion gap.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong>, <strong>C</strong>, <strong>D</strong> and <strong>E</strong> cause <strong>normal anion gap (hyperchloraemic)</strong> acidosis.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-ab-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_8"],"note":"acid-base","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient has a PaCO<sub>2</sub> of 8.0 kPa and a bicarbonate of 24 mmol/L.</p>\n<p>Using the Henderson–Hasselbalch equation (pKa 6.1, CO<sub>2</sub> solubility 0.23 mmol/L/kPa), what is the approximate pH?</p>","options":["7.05","7.11","7.21","7.29","7.40"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>pH = 6.1 + log<sub>10</sub> (24 / (0.23 × 8.0)) = 6.1 + log<sub>10</sub> (24 / 1.84) = 6.1 + log<sub>10</sub> (13.0) ≈ 6.1 + 1.11 = <strong>7.21</strong>. This is an acute respiratory acidosis with little renal compensation yet.</p>"},{"id":"ph-fl-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_14"],"note":"fluids-osmolality","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 70 kg adult receives 1 L of 5% glucose intravenously.</p>\n<p>Assuming the glucose is metabolised and water distributes freely, approximately how much of the infused volume remains in the plasma?</p>","options":["25 mL","70 mL","250 mL","700 mL","1000 mL"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Once the glucose is metabolised, the infusion is <strong>free water</strong>, which distributes across total body water (~42 L). Plasma (~3 L) is about 7% of total body water, so about <strong>70 mL</strong> stays intravascular.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>C</strong> (~250 mL, a quarter) is the approximate figure for an isotonic crystalloid distributing across the ECF.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-fl-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G3_67","1_GA_F2_7"],"note":"fluids-osmolality","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>How many milligrams of lidocaine are contained in 10 mL of a 2% solution?</p>","options":["2 mg","20 mg","100 mg","200 mg","2000 mg"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>1% = 10 mg/mL, so 2% = <strong>20 mg/mL</strong>; 10 mL contains <strong>200 mg</strong>.</p>"},{"id":"ph-hb-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_19"],"note":"blood-rbc-hb","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient given a large dose of prilocaine for a Bier's block becomes cyanosed. SpO<sub>2</sub> reads 85% despite 100% oxygen, and the arterial PaO<sub>2</sub> is 45 kPa.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate specific treatment?</p>","options":["Hydroxocobalamin","Hyperbaric oxygen","Intravenous lipid emulsion","Methylthioninium chloride (methylene blue)","Sodium bicarbonate"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>The metabolite <strong>o-toluidine</strong> oxidises haemoglobin to <strong>methaemoglobin</strong> (Fe<sup>3+</sup>), which cannot carry oxygen and makes pulse oximeters read about 85%. Methylthioninium chloride (1–2 mg/kg IV) reduces it via NADPH-methaemoglobin reductase; avoid in G6PD deficiency.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> treats cyanide poisoning.</li><li><strong>B</strong> is for severe carbon monoxide poisoning.</li><li><strong>C</strong> treats local anaesthetic systemic toxicity (cardiovascular or CNS), which this is not.</li><li><strong>E</strong> is not a treatment for methaemoglobinaemia.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-hb-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_20"],"note":"blood-rbc-hb","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with rheumatoid arthritis has Hb 105 g/L, ferritin 180 µg/L (normal range 30–300), transferrin saturation 12% and a CRP of 45 mg/L.</p>\n<p>Which is the most likely explanation?</p>","options":["Folate deficiency","Functional iron deficiency with the anaemia of inflammation","Haemolytic anaemia","Iron overload","Vitamin B<sub>12</sub> deficiency"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Ferritin is an <strong>acute phase protein</strong>, so a normal ferritin does not exclude iron deficiency when CRP is raised. A <strong>low transferrin saturation</strong> (&lt; 20%) with inflammation indicates <strong>functional iron deficiency</strong>: hepcidin blocks iron release and absorption. IV iron may help before major surgery.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> and <strong>E</strong> cause macrocytosis; the iron studies point to iron-restricted erythropoiesis.</li><li><strong>C</strong> would show haemolysis markers.</li><li><strong>D</strong> would give high ferritin and high transferrin saturation.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-coag-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_24","1_GA_G3_63"],"note":"blood-haemostasis","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which group of coagulation factors depends on vitamin K for its synthesis?</p>","options":["I, II, V, VIII","II, VII, IX, X","III, V, VII, XII","V, VIII, XI, XIII","VIII, IX, XI, XII"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Factors <strong>II, VII, IX and X</strong> (plus proteins C and S) need vitamin K-dependent γ-carboxylation. Warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase; factor VII (shortest half-life, about 6 h) falls first, so the PT/INR rises first.</p>"},{"id":"ph-coag-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_24","1_GA_F3_24"],"note":"blood-haemostasis","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During a postpartum haemorrhage, a viscoelastic test shows a normal clotting time but a greatly reduced clot firmness in the test that inhibits platelets (FIBTEM).</p>\n<p>Which treatment is most appropriate on this result?</p>","options":["Fibrinogen replacement (fibrinogen concentrate or cryoprecipitate)","Fresh frozen plasma alone","Platelet transfusion","Protamine","Vitamin K"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Low <strong>FIBTEM</strong> clot firmness indicates a <strong>low functional fibrinogen</strong>, the key early coagulopathy of obstetric haemorrhage. Replace fibrinogen.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>B</strong> FFP contains relatively little fibrinogen and dilutes.</li><li><strong>C</strong> Platelet function is assessed by comparing EXTEM and FIBTEM; FIBTEM alone points to fibrinogen.</li><li><strong>D</strong> reverses heparin; the clotting time is normal.</li><li><strong>E</strong> has no immediate effect.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-tx-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_22","1_GA_G2_23"],"note":"blood-transfusion","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A group A RhD-positive patient needs plasma urgently.</p>\n<p>Which plasma group is compatible?</p>","options":["Group A or AB","Group B only","Group B or O","Group O only","Any group, because plasma contains no red cells"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Plasma <strong>contains antibodies</strong>. Group A plasma contains anti-B, which is compatible with a group A recipient; <strong>AB plasma</strong> contains no ABO antibodies and is the universal plasma donor.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>B</strong>, <strong>C</strong> and <strong>D</strong> contain anti-A, which would react with the patient's A cells.</li><li><strong>E</strong> ignores the antibodies in plasma.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-tx-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_E_19"],"note":"blood-transfusion","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Two hours into a transfusion of a second unit of red cells, an 84-year-old woman with heart failure becomes breathless and hypertensive, with bilateral crackles. She responds to furosemide.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>","options":["Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction","Anaphylaxis","Febrile non-haemolytic reaction","Transfusion-associated circulatory overload (TACO)","Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI)"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p><strong>TACO</strong>: pulmonary oedema with <strong>hypertension</strong>, in an elderly patient with cardiac disease, improving with diuretics.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>E</strong> TRALI causes non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, typically with <strong>hypotension</strong>, fever, and no response to diuretics.</li><li><strong>A</strong>, <strong>B</strong> and <strong>C</strong> do not fit this picture.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-imm-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G3_23","1_GA_E_18"],"note":"immunity-anaphylaxis","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient has a suspected anaphylactic reaction five minutes after induction. Resuscitation is successful.</p>\n<p>When should mast cell tryptase samples ideally be taken?</p>","options":["A single sample 24 hours later","As soon as feasible, again at about 1–2 hours, and a baseline at least 24 hours later","Before any adrenaline is given","Only if the patient required cardiopulmonary resuscitation","Within 5 minutes and again at 6 hours"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Tryptase peaks about <strong>1 hour</strong> after the reaction (half-life about 2 hours). Take an initial sample once resuscitation allows, one at around 1–2 hours, and a <strong>baseline at least 24 hours later</strong> to compare. Never delay treatment to take samples.</p>"},{"id":"ph-imm-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_27"],"note":"immunity-anaphylaxis","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia is best classified as which type of hypersensitivity reaction?</p>","options":["Type I (IgE-mediated)","Type II (antibody-mediated cytotoxic)","Type III (immune complex)","Type IV (T cell-mediated)","Non-immune direct mast cell activation"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>In HIT, <strong>IgG antibodies</strong> bind heparin–platelet factor 4 complexes on platelets, activating and removing them: an <strong>antibody-mediated (type II)</strong> reaction that paradoxically causes thrombosis.</p>"},{"id":"ph-endo-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_79","1_GA_G2_80"],"note":"endocrine-stress-glucose","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which hormonal change is characteristic of the stress response to major abdominal surgery?</p>","options":["Decreased antidiuretic hormone","Decreased cortisol","Increased insulin sensitivity","Increased glucagon with relative insulin deficiency and resistance","Increased thyroxine production"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Surgery activates the hypothalamic–pituitary and sympathetic systems: <strong>catecholamines, cortisol and glucagon rise</strong>, insulin is relatively deficient and tissues become <strong>insulin resistant</strong>, producing hyperglycaemia and catabolism. ADH also rises.</p>"},{"id":"ph-endo-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_85","1_POM_C_3"],"note":"endocrine-glands","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient on long-term prednisolone 15 mg daily develops hypotension unresponsive to vasopressors during emergency surgery.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate immediate drug treatment for the likely cause?</p>","options":["Dexamethasone","Fludrocortisone","Hydrocortisone","Levothyroxine","Vasopressin"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Long-term steroids suppress the hypothalamic–pituitary–adrenal axis, so the patient cannot mount a cortisol stress response: <strong>acute adrenal insufficiency</strong>. Give <strong>IV hydrocortisone</strong> (glucocorticoid with mineralocorticoid activity).</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> has no mineralocorticoid activity and is less appropriate for crisis.</li><li><strong>B</strong> is oral mineralocorticoid replacement for chronic Addison's disease.</li><li><strong>D</strong> is irrelevant here.</li><li><strong>E</strong> may support blood pressure but does not treat the cause.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-temp-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_81","1_GA_F2_5"],"note":"thermoregulation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What is the main reason core temperature falls by about 1°C in the first hour after induction of general anaesthesia?</p>","options":["Cold intravenous fluids","Evaporation from the respiratory tract","Redistribution of heat from the core to the periphery","Reduced basal metabolic rate","Radiation to the theatre environment"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Anaesthesia abolishes tonic thermoregulatory vasoconstriction, so heat flows from the warm core to the cooler peripheral tissues: <strong>redistribution</strong>. Pre-warming the periphery reduces it. Radiation is the largest route of heat <strong>loss</strong> to the environment, but the early fall is mainly redistribution.</p>"},{"id":"ph-gi-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_71","1_GA_G3_72"],"note":"liver-gi","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>The chemoreceptor trigger zone lies in the area postrema.</p>\n<p>Which feature explains why it detects circulating emetogenic drugs such as opioids?</p>","options":["It contains only histamine receptors","It is supplied by the vestibular nerve","It lies outside the blood–brain barrier","It receives vagal afferents from the stomach","It is inhibited by dopamine"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The area postrema is a <strong>circumventricular organ without an effective blood–brain barrier</strong>, so it samples blood and CSF directly. It has D<sub>2</sub>, 5-HT<sub>3</sub>, NK<sub>1</sub> and opioid receptors.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>E</strong> Dopamine <strong>stimulates</strong> it (hence D<sub>2</sub> antagonists are antiemetic).</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-preg-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_89"],"note":"pregnancy-physiology","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which set of arterial blood gas values is most typical of a healthy woman at 36 weeks' gestation breathing air?</p>","options":["pH 7.32, PaCO<sub>2</sub> 6.0 kPa, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 23 mmol/L","pH 7.40, PaCO<sub>2</sub> 5.3 kPa, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 24 mmol/L","pH 7.44, PaCO<sub>2</sub> 4.1 kPa, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 20 mmol/L","pH 7.52, PaCO<sub>2</sub> 3.5 kPa, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 24 mmol/L","pH 7.44, PaCO<sub>2</sub> 5.3 kPa, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 28 mmol/L"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Progesterone increases minute ventilation, so PaCO<sub>2</sub> falls to about 4 kPa. The kidneys compensate by excreting bicarbonate (about 18–21 mmol/L), keeping pH near normal or slightly alkaline: a <strong>compensated respiratory alkalosis</strong>.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>D</strong> is uncompensated.</li><li><strong>A</strong>, <strong>B</strong> and <strong>E</strong> do not reflect the changes of pregnancy.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-preg-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_Q_3"],"note":"pregnancy-physiology","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A woman at 34 weeks' gestation becomes pale, nauseated and hypotensive while lying supine on the operating table before a caesarean section under spinal anaesthesia.</p>\n<p>What is the most important immediate action?</p>","options":["Give a 500 mL crystalloid bolus","Give ephedrine 6 mg IV","Lower the head of the table","Tilt the table or manually displace the uterus to the left","Start an oxytocin infusion"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>This is <strong>aortocaval compression</strong> (supine hypotensive syndrome), made worse by sympathetic block. Relieve the compression first with <strong>left uterine displacement</strong> or table tilt; then use vasopressors (phenylephrine infusion is standard) and fluids as needed.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> and <strong>B</strong> may be needed but do not treat the cause.</li><li><strong>C</strong> worsens compression and risks a high block.</li><li><strong>E</strong> is given after delivery.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-paed-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_O_1","1_GA_P_5"],"note":"paeds-physiology","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What is the hourly maintenance fluid requirement for a 24 kg child according to the 4-2-1 rule?</p>","options":["44 mL/h","54 mL/h","64 mL/h","74 mL/h","96 mL/h"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>4 mL/kg/h for the first 10 kg (40 mL) + 2 mL/kg/h for the next 10 kg (20 mL) + 1 mL/kg/h for the remaining 4 kg (4 mL) = <strong>64 mL/h</strong>. Use an isotonic fluid.</p>"},{"id":"ph-paed-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_O_1"],"note":"paeds-physiology","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 6-month-old infant becomes bradycardic during a gas induction.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely cause, and why does it matter more than in an adult?</p>","options":["Hypervolaemia; infants have a large blood volume per kilogram","Hypoxia; infant cardiac output is largely heart rate-dependent","Hypothermia; infants rely on shivering","Hyperkalaemia; infants have immature kidneys","Vagal stimulation from the facemask; infants have high sympathetic tone"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Infants have high oxygen consumption and a small FRC, so they desaturate quickly, and <strong>bradycardia is commonly a sign of hypoxia</strong>. Their stroke volume is relatively fixed, so <strong>cardiac output falls with heart rate</strong>. Oxygenate first.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>C</strong> Infants rely on <strong>non-shivering</strong> thermogenesis.</li><li><strong>E</strong> Infants have dominant <strong>parasympathetic</strong> tone; vagal reflexes occur, but hypoxia must be excluded first.</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-age-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_75","1_GA_I_1"],"note":"ageing-obesity","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 1.70 m, 150 kg man is to have a rapid sequence induction.</p>\n<p>Which weight is most appropriate for calculating the dose of suxamethonium?</p>","options":["Adjusted body weight","Ideal body weight","Lean body weight","Total body weight","Weight is irrelevant; a fixed dose is used"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>In obesity, <strong>plasma cholinesterase activity and ECF volume increase</strong>, so suxamethonium (1–1.5 mg/kg) is dosed on <strong>total body weight</strong> to ensure good intubating conditions.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>B</strong> is used for non-depolarising neuromuscular blockers.</li><li><strong>C</strong> is used for induction agents and opioids.</li><li><strong>A</strong> is used for some other drugs (e.g. certain antibiotics).</li></ul>"},{"id":"ph-age-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_2"],"note":"ageing-obesity","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>An 80-year-old patient is anaesthetised with sevoflurane.</p>\n<p>Compared with a 40-year-old, by approximately how much is the MAC reduced?</p>","options":["0%","10%","25%","50%","75%"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>MAC falls by about <strong>6% per decade</strong> after 40 years: 4 decades × 6% ≈ <strong>24%</strong>.</p>"},{"id":"ph-met-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_77","1_GA_G2_78"],"note":"nutrition-metabolism","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A ventilated patient in intensive care receives parenteral nutrition with a very high glucose load. Indirect calorimetry shows a respiratory quotient of 1.2.</p>\n<p>What is the main clinical consequence?</p>","options":["Increased CO<sub>2</sub> production making weaning harder","Increased ketone production","Metabolic alkalosis","Reduced oxygen consumption","Respiratory quotient has no clinical relevance"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>An RQ above 1 indicates <strong>lipogenesis from excess carbohydrate</strong> (overfeeding), which generates extra CO<sub>2</sub> and increases ventilatory demand, making weaning harder.</p>"},{"id":"ph-met-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_10","1_GA_G2_78"],"note":"nutrition-metabolism","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with severe asthma is receiving nebulised salbutamol and an adrenaline infusion. Lactate is 5.2 mmol/L with good peripheral perfusion and normal ScvO<sub>2</sub>.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely mechanism of the raised lactate?</p>","options":["Hepatic failure","Metformin accumulation","Stimulation of glycolysis through β<sub>2</sub> receptors","Tissue hypoxia from shock","Thiamine deficiency"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>β<sub>2</sub>-agonists and adrenaline stimulate glycolysis and pyruvate production, increasing lactate without tissue hypoxia: a <strong>type B</strong> lactic acidosis.</p>"},{"id":"ph-cell-001","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_12","1_GA_G3_18"],"note":"cell-physiology","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient's plasma phenytoin concentration rises from 10 to 30 mg/L after only a small increase in the daily dose.</p>\n<p>Which pharmacological principle best explains this?</p>","options":["Enzyme induction","First-order elimination","Increased protein binding","Saturation of metabolising enzymes (zero-order kinetics)","Reduced volume of distribution"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>At therapeutic concentrations, phenytoin's hepatic enzymes approach <strong>saturation</strong> (Michaelis–Menten kinetics). Once saturated, a constant <strong>amount</strong> is eliminated per unit time (zero-order), so small dose increases produce disproportionate rises in concentration.</p>"},{"id":"ph-cell-002","subject":"physiology","codes":["1_GA_G2_17"],"note":"cell-physiology","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Glucose absorption from the proximal renal tubule into tubular cells against its concentration gradient is an example of which transport mechanism?</p>","options":["Endocytosis","Facilitated diffusion via GLUT2","Primary active transport","Secondary active transport","Simple diffusion"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p><strong>SGLT</strong> co-transporters move glucose with Na<sup>+</sup> down the sodium gradient created by the basolateral <strong>Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase</strong>: secondary active transport. Glucose then leaves the cell by facilitated diffusion (GLUT2) on the other side.</p>"},{"id":"ps-001","subject":"statistics","codes":["1_RD_E_4"],"note":"stats-data-descriptive","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>The ASA physical status grade of patients in a study is an example of which type of data?</p>","options":["Continuous ratio data","Discrete numerical data","Interval data","Nominal categorical data","Ordinal categorical data"],"answer":4,"explain":"<p>ASA grades are <strong>ordered categories</strong> with unequal, undefined intervals: <strong>ordinal</strong> data, summarised by median and interquartile range and analysed with non-parametric tests.</p>"},{"id":"ps-002","subject":"statistics","codes":["1_RD_E_6"],"note":"stats-data-descriptive","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>The length of hospital stay after surgery in a cohort has a long tail to the right.</p>\n<p>Which statement is most likely to be true?</p>","options":["Mean = median = mode","Mean is greater than the median","Median is greater than the mean","The data should be summarised with mean and standard deviation","The standard error of the mean equals the standard deviation"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>In a <strong>positively (right) skewed</strong> distribution, a few very long stays pull the <strong>mean above the median</strong>. The median and interquartile range are more appropriate summaries.</p>"},{"id":"ps-003","subject":"statistics","codes":["1_RD_E_6","1_RD_E_7"],"note":"stats-data-descriptive","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>In a sample of 100 patients, systolic blood pressure has a mean of 130 mmHg and a standard deviation of 20 mmHg.</p>\n<p>What is the approximate 95% confidence interval for the population mean?</p>","options":["90 to 170 mmHg","110 to 150 mmHg","126 to 134 mmHg","128 to 132 mmHg","129.6 to 130.4 mmHg"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>SEM = SD / √n = 20 / 10 = 2. The 95% CI ≈ mean ± 1.96 × SEM ≈ 130 ± 3.9 = <strong>about 126 to 134 mmHg</strong>.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong> is roughly the range covering 95% of <strong>individual</strong> values (mean ± 2 SD).</li></ul>"},{"id":"ps-004","subject":"statistics","codes":["1_RD_E_8","1_RD_E_10"],"note":"stats-hypothesis-tests","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A trial concludes that a new antiemetic is no better than placebo, but it only recruited 40 patients. In fact, the drug is effective.</p>\n<p>What error has occurred?</p>","options":["Bias","Confounding","Publication bias","Type I error","Type II error"],"answer":4,"explain":"<p>Failing to detect a real effect (a <strong>false negative</strong>) is a <strong>type II error</strong> (β), typically from an underpowered study.</p>"},{"id":"ps-005","subject":"statistics","codes":["1_RD_E_9"],"note":"stats-hypothesis-tests","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Pain scores (0–10 numerical rating scale) are compared between two independent groups of patients receiving different analgesic regimens.</p>\n<p>Which test is most appropriate?</p>","options":["Chi-squared test","Mann–Whitney U test","Paired t-test","Pearson correlation","Wilcoxon signed-rank test"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Pain scores are <strong>ordinal</strong> and the groups are <strong>independent</strong>, so a <strong>non-parametric test for two independent groups</strong> — Mann–Whitney U — is appropriate. Wilcoxon signed-rank is for paired data.</p>"},{"id":"ps-006","subject":"statistics","codes":["1_RD_E_9"],"note":"stats-hypothesis-tests","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A new screening test for difficult intubation is positive in 40 of 50 patients who proved difficult, and in 90 of 950 patients who were easy.</p>\n<p>What is its sensitivity?</p>","options":["9%","31%","80%","91%","98%"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Sensitivity = true positives / all with the condition = 40/50 = <strong>80%</strong>. Specificity = 860/950 ≈ 91%. PPV = 40/130 ≈ 31% — low because the condition is uncommon.</p>"},{"id":"ps-007","subject":"statistics","codes":["1_RD_E_2","1_RD_E_3"],"note":"stats-hypothesis-tests","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>In a trial, postoperative pneumonia occurs in 10% of the control group and 6% of the treatment group.</p>\n<p>What is the number needed to treat to prevent one pneumonia?</p>","options":["4","6","10","25","40"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Absolute risk reduction = 10% − 6% = 4% = 0.04. <strong>NNT = 1/0.04 = 25.</strong> (The relative risk reduction is 40%.)</p>"},{"id":"ps-008","subject":"statistics","codes":["1_RD_E_1","1_SQI_C_1"],"note":"stats-study-design","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A department measures the proportion of patients who are normothermic on arrival in recovery each week, tries a series of small changes, and plots the results over time on a run chart.</p>\n<p>What is this activity best described as?</p>","options":["Audit","Case–control study","Quality improvement using PDSA cycles","Randomised controlled trial","Service evaluation of a new drug"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Iterative <strong>small tests of change</strong> measured frequently over time (run charts) using <strong>data for improvement</strong> is <strong>quality improvement</strong> (Model for Improvement, PDSA). An audit compares practice against a standard and re-audits.</p>"}];
